{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_62","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'git status --short\ngit check-ignore -v cores/bench/implementation_notes.md\ngit diff --stat\ncat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md'","aggregated_output":" M cores/bench/rtl/alu.sv\n M cores/bench/test/test_alu.py\n.gitignore:87:cores/*/implementation_notes.md\tcores/bench/implementation_notes.md\n cores/bench/rtl/alu.sv       | 57 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-----------\n cores/bench/test/test_alu.py | 28 +++++++++++++++++++++-\n 2 files changed, 70 insertions(+), 15 deletions(-)\n# Divider feedback by divisor magnitude\n\nThe divider classifies the unsigned divisor magnitude in round one, alongside registration of 3D, 5D and 7D. For nonzero magnitudes below `0x20000`, the two-bit opening round seeds a 17-bit remainder. Each of the five remaining rounds consumes six bits using two radix-8 digits with 20-bit inputs and 21-bit trial differences. At or above the cutoff, the upper 17 dividend bits already form a valid remainder. Five full-width radix-8 digits consume the remaining 15 bits.\n\nEach path has its own 32-bit quotient shift register and remainder register. Both advance in parallel. The narrow quotient register also holds the issue-time dividend magnitude for initializing both paths. The result mux selects the quotient or remainder and applies the captured sign. A zero divisor selects the original dividend for REM or all ones for DIV. Both schedules assert done after the sixth round.\n\n## Local verification\n\n- Verilator lint passed with `-Wall`.\n- All 92 cocotb cases passed. The new test checks 280 consecutive requests that alternate divisor widths across `0x1ffff`, `0x20000` and `0x20001`. It covers both signs, maximum dividends, exact multiples and their neighbors, and checks busy and done on the transfer edge.\n- The single-retirement ALTOPS formal suite passed all 53 checks on its first run.\n- Co-simulation passed for selftest and three random programs, each with and without bus stalls. Stalled CoreMark matched all five CRCs and reported correct operation. Its markers were at cycles 15,034 and 4,121,650, giving 4,106,616 bracketed cycles for ten iterations.\n\n## Timing and area\n\nGowin placement options 0, 1 and 2 reached 73.746, 83.943 and 83.943 MHz. The median gives **204.41 CoreMark iterations/s**, a **4.69% gain** over 195.25 iterations/s. Its 41-cell worst path runs from the MEM/WB result selector through writeback and EX to the fetch PC.\n\nThe parallel datapaths raise utilization from 3,872 to 3,974 logic cells and from 803 to 886 registers. Placement option 0 falls below the 83.88 MHz target. Reusing the narrow quotient register for the dividend magnitude gave the best median among the input-register and result-mux arrangements tested.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
