{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_10", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/core.yaml; cat cores/bench/CORE_PHILOSOPHY.md; tail -70 cores/bench/LESSONS.md'", "aggregated_output": "name: bench\ndescription: Frozen LLM-benchmark fixture core. Identical RTL to baseline at the bench-fixture-v1\n  freeze point. The benchmark matrix clones from the bench-fixture-v1 orphan branch\n  and operates only on this directory.\nisa: rv32im\nnret: 1\ntarget_fpga: tang-nano-20k\ntargets:\n  fmax_mhz: 135\n  lut4: 10000\n  coremark_iter_s: 300\n  coremark_per_mhz: 2.2\ncurrent:\n  fmax_mhz: 81.702\n  lut4: 4667\n  ff: 1392\n  coremark_iter_s: 3.0e-06\n  coremark_per_mhz: 0.0\n  source_id: hyp-20261119-001-r8s0\n  updated: '2026-11-19T18:38:38.668531Z'\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-001-r1s0 (regression, +476.16%): CoreMark executes no divide or remainder between its timing markers, so chaining eight restoring steps per cycle saved no benchmark cycles over six and lowered median Gowin Fmax from 43.1 to 31.4 MHz.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-002-r1s1 (improvement, +692.41%): Replacing combinational DIV/REM in EX with a shared six-round restoring divider shortened the critical path and raised median Gowin Fmax from 5.44 to 43.1 MHz, while timed CoreMark incurred no divider stalls because it executed neither operation.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-003-r1s2 (regression, +7.76%): Correctly predicting a taken branch or JAL in fetch avoided a one-cycle EX redirect bubble, but the combinational DIV/REM path still limited CoreMark throughput to 13.06 iterations/s versus 96.04 with the six-round divider.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-001-r2s0 (regression, +10.42%): Replacing six serial restoring steps with three radix-4 digits per full round raised median Gowin Fmax from 43.1 to 47.6 MHz at the same CoreMark cycle count and shifted the critical path from remainder_q feedback to the register-file read through EX to data memory.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-002-r2s1 (improvement, +14.16%): Registering 3D, 5D and 7D during the two-bit opening round let each later round resolve six quotient bits in two radix-8 stages of parallel trial subtraction and selection, raising median Gowin Fmax from 43.1 to 49.2 MHz at the same CoreMark cycle count.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp\n…[4308 of 7380 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:a9b1b69e827b0912]…\nterations versus the same predictor with replay triggered only by short backedges.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-001-r8s0 (improvement, +8.28%): Packing each PC tag with its instruction in a 512-row block RAM limited a conflicting replay fill to one eviction, while parallel reads at the registered normal and recovery PCs kept the recovery mux off both RAM address paths.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-002-r8s1 (regression, +7.48%): Reading successor RAM during the predecessor’s fetch let a PC-matched word cover instruction-bus waits even across predicted jumps, cutting prototype CoreMark cycles by 8.03%, though final throughput trailed the 512-entry replay table at 232.01 versus 233.74 iterations/s.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-003-r8s2 (regression, +6.78%): Comparing EX rd with rs1 and rs2 of the bus, successor and replay words in parallel with the successor PC check kept the instruction mux off the load-use hazard path, raising median Fmax from 65.969 to 77.982 MHz at the same CoreMark cycle count in paired prototypes.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_9", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '530,920p' cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py\"", "aggregated_output": "                return 1, 1\n            return 0, 1\n        if ia == 0x148 and visits[0x140] == 5 and exit_waits < 3:\n            # The fall-through was recorded on entry. The untaken exit\n            # must still wait for the bus after consuming the loop tail.\n            exit_waits += 1\n            return 0, 1\n        return 1, 1\n\n    rets, _ = await _run(dut, program, bus_ready=ready)\n    pcs = ([0, 4] + [0x3c, 0x40, 0x44] * 2 + [0x48, 0x148, 0x14c, 0x144]\n           + [0x13c, 0x140, 0x144] * 2 + [0x148, 0x150])\n    _check_loop_path(rets, pcs)\n    assert visits == {0x13c: 5, 0x140: 5} and exit_waits == 3\n    assert [r[\"rd_wdata\"] for r in rets if r[\"rd\"] == 3] == [3, 6, 15, 26]\n\n\n@cocotb.test()\nasync def replay_word_residency_and_replacement(dut):\n    # Visit both bodies twice. Each short backedge enables replay on entry.\n    # The final jump shares the row at 32, leaving the store at 36 resident.\n    program = [NOP()] * (2080 // 4 + 1)\n    for pc, instr in {\n        0: ADDI(3, 0, 3), 4: ADDI(4, 0, 4), 8: JAL(0, 32 - 8),\n        32: ADDI(1, 1, 1), 36: SW(1, 0, 64), 40: BEQ(1, 3, 12),\n        44: JAL(0, 304 - 44), 48: BEQ(0, 0, -16), 52: EBREAK(),\n        288: ADDI(2, 2, 2), 292: SW(2, 0, 68), 296: BEQ(2, 4, 2080 - 296),\n        300: JAL(0, 48 - 300), 304: BEQ(0, 0, -16),\n        2080: JAL(0, 48 - 2080),\n    }.items():\n        program[pc // 4] = instr\n\n    stores, probes = [], []\n    replaced = False\n    refill_cycles = 0\n\n    def ready(ia, da, ren, wen, wdata):\n        nonlocal replaced, refill_cycles\n        replaced |= ia == 2080\n        if wen:\n            stores.append((da, wen, wdata))\n        if len(stores) >= 2 and ia in (32, 36, 288, 292):\n            probes.append(ia)\n            if replaced and ia == 32:\n                refill_cycles += 1\n                return refill_cycles == 4, 1\n            return 0, 1\n        return 1, 1\n\n    rets, _ = await _run(dut, program, bus_ready=ready)\n    lap = [32, 36, 40, 44, 304, 288, 292, 296]\n    pcs = [0, 4, 8] + lap + [300, 48] + lap + [2080, 48, 32, 36, 40, 52]\n\n…[11608 of 14680 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:f2d12a083799a8bd]…\n\n    ]\n\n\n@cocotb.test()\nasync def jal_writes_pc_plus_4(dut):\n    \"\"\"JAL x1, +8; verify the wrong-path slot does not retire and x1=PC+4.\"\"\"\n    program = [\n        JAL(1, 8),             # PC=0; x1 := 4; jump to PC=8\n        ADDI(2, 0, 99),        # PC=4 — wrong-path; must not retire\n        ADDI(3, 0, 42),        # PC=8 — target\n        EBREAK(),\n    ]\n    rets, _ = await _run(dut, program)\n    pcs = [r[\"pc\"] for r in rets]\n    assert pcs == [0, 8, 12], f\"unexpected retirement PC sequence: {pcs}\"\n    [rj] = _by_insn(rets, JAL(1, 8))\n    assert rj[\"rd\"] == 1\n    assert rj[\"rd_wdata\"] == rj[\"pc\"] + 4\n    [r3] = _by_insn(rets, ADDI(3, 0, 42))\n    assert r3[\"rd_wdata\"] == 42\n\n\n@cocotb.test()\nasync def jalr_target(dut):\n    \"\"\"ADDI x5,x0,12; JALR x1,x5,0; verify wrong-path slot doesn't retire.\"\"\"\n    program = [\n        ADDI(5, 0, 12),        # PC=0;  x5 := 12\n        JALR(1, 5, 0),         # PC=4;  jump to x5+0 = 12\n        ADDI(6, 0, 99),        # PC=8;  wrong-path\n        ADDI(7, 0, 42),        # PC=12; target\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat tools/eval/cosim.py; cat cores/bench/test/_helpers.py; ls -la bench/programs | head -30; nproc; cat /sys/fs/cgroup/cpu.max; cat /sys/fs/cgroup/memory.max'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_11", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat tools/eval/cosim.py; cat cores/bench/test/_helpers.py; ls -la bench/programs | head -30; nproc; cat /sys/fs/cgroup/cpu.max; cat /sys/fs/cgroup/memory.max'", "aggregated_output": "\"\"\"Runs Verilator cosim for all bench ELFs. Returns structured pass/fail.\n\nCorrectness is gated in two stages:\n  1. Full RVFI trace cosim for selftest and any other small ELF — every\n     retirement is diffed against the Python reference ISS.\n  2. CoreMark CRC validation via the sim's UART capture — full-trace cosim\n     of coremark.elf is skipped because the Python reference is ~10⁴× slower\n     than Verilator (would take 30+ minutes per candidate). Instead we run\n     the sim in --bench mode and verify its UART matches the canonical CRCs\n     with `validate_coremark_uart` (the same check run_fpga_eval uses). This\n     catches any CPU bug that affects CoreMark output without the full trace\n     overhead.\n\"\"\"\nimport os, subprocess, json, sys\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom concurrent.futures import ThreadPoolExecutor, as_completed\n\nfrom tools.eval.formal import read_nret\nfrom tools.eval._subprocess import run_pgroup\n\nBENCH_DIR = Path(\"bench/programs\")\n\n\ndef _build_cosim_env(worktree, target: str | None,\n                     base_env: dict | None = None) -> dict:\n    \"\"\"Build the env for `bash test/cosim/build.sh`.\n\n    Reads cores/<target>/core.yaml's `nret` (defaults to 2) and injects\n    NRET=<n> so build.sh can pass `-DNRET=<n>` to verilator. main.cpp's\n    channel-1 drain is compiled out under `#if NRET < 2`, so a single-\n    issue core (no `_1` ports) builds cleanly.\n    \"\"\"\n    worktree_path = Path(worktree).resolve()\n    env = dict(base_env) if base_env is not None else os.environ.copy()\n    if target is None:\n        return env\n    env[\"RTL_DIR\"] = f\"cores/{target}/rtl\"\n    env[\"OBJ_DIR\"] = f\"cores/{target}/obj_dir\"\n    env[\"NRET\"] = str(read_nret(worktree_path / \"cores\" / target / \"core.yaml\"))\n    return env\n\n# Stall modes for the full-trace comparison. The stalled mode uses the same\n# bus backpressure as the fitness run, so logic that only acts while a\n# memory port stalls (which V2 now times and scores) is trace-checked too.\nTRACE_STALL_MODES = ((), (\"--istall\", \"--dstall\"))\n\n\ndef run_one(elf: Path, sim\n…[7961 of 11033 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:e538554fcae21d5e]…\nuild_dir),\n        always=True,\n        build_args=[\"-Wall\", \"-Wno-fatal\", \"-Wno-style\"],\n    )\n    runner.test(\n        hdl_toplevel=toplevel,\n        test_module=test_module,\n        test_dir=str(TEST_DIR),\n        build_dir=str(build_dir),\n    )\ntotal 464\ndrwxrwx---+  3 nobody nogroup   4096 Nov 19 19:59 .\ndrwxrwx---+  3 nobody nogroup   4096 Nov 19 19:59 ..\n-rw-rw----+  1 nobody nogroup   2317 Nov 19 19:59 Makefile\ndrwxrwx---+ 14 nobody nogroup   4096 Nov 19 19:59 coremark\n-rw-rw----+  1 nobody nogroup    135 Nov 19 19:59 crt0.S\n-rw-rw----+  1 nobody nogroup   6341 Nov 19 19:59 gen_random_trace.py\n-rw-rw----+  1 nobody nogroup    330 Nov 19 19:59 link.ld\n-rw-rw----+  1 nobody nogroup 140494 Nov 19 19:59 random1.S\n-rw-rw----+  1 nobody nogroup 138125 Nov 19 19:59 random2.S\n-rw-rw----+  1 nobody nogroup 142438 Nov 19 19:59 random3.S\n-rw-rw----+  1 nobody nogroup   3684 Nov 19 19:59 selftest.S\n20\ncat: /sys/fs/cgroup/cpu.max: No such file or directory\ncat: /sys/fs/cgroup/memory.max: No such file or directory\n", "exit_code": 1, "status": "failed"}}
