{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_48","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md'","aggregated_output":"# EX-cycle target fetch\n\nAn aligned taken branch, JAL or JALR drives the instruction address in EX. ID samples the selected PC and instruction together. IF records that PC as its fetch base. An increment flag selects the following word after acceptance or the same target after a stall. Back-to-back transfers can therefore retire on consecutive cycles.\n\nThe implementation shares one resolved target and alignment check between fetch, the misalignment trap and RVFI next-PC reporting. JALR clears bit zero before the check. A misaligned taken transfer retires with a trap, suppresses the link write and continues at PC+4.\n\nID/EX holds during a blocked data access or divider wait. On an advancing edge, fetch readiness and the load-use interlock determine ID/EX validity. EX masks controls from invalid entries and divider bubbles. The held WB bypass keeps branch operands available throughout a data stall.\n\n## Local verification\n\n- Verilator passed the requested lint command with zero warnings.\n- All 53 single-retire ALTOPS formal checks passed on the final logic, with zero failures or corrective retries.\n- All 85 cocotb cases passed. The pipeline tests cover consecutive branch and target retirements, a dependent JAL-to-JALR chain and both link writes, target retry, a branch held behind a store, single store acceptance, and misaligned transfers.\n- Selftest and all three random programs matched the reference RVFI trace with both buses ready and with random stalls.\n- Canonical 2K CoreMark completed 10 iterations. All five CRCs matched, UART reported “Correct operation validated.” and the simulation reported zero out-of-bounds accesses.\n\n## Performance\n\nThe start and stop writes occurred at cycles 14,674 and 4,150,770. The resulting **4,136,096 bracketed cycles** match the prototype and save 355,389 cycles (7.91%) against the champion.\n\n| Gowin placement option | Fmax (MHz) |\n| --- | ---: |\n| 0 | 48.397 |\n| 1 | 50.052 |\n| 2 | 50.052 |\n\nThe 50.052 MHz median gives **121.01 iterations/s**, 10.37% above 109.64 iterations/s. All three placements clear the 47.4342 MHz acceptance threshold. Their worst reported setup paths run from register-file storage through EX to data memory.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
