{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_37","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'git diff --check; cat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md; git status --short --untracked-files=all'","aggregated_output":"# Decode operand preparation\n\nDecode reads two 16×32 distributed RAM banks and compares each source tag with the ID/EX and EX/MEM destination tags in parallel. The existing ID/EX operand registers and the four new match bits share the same advance condition. A reset clears the register file's written bitmap. Each read port gives x0 and a matching WB write priority over a bank read.\n\nEX qualifies the registered matches with the current producer write controls and gives EX/MEM priority. The MEM/WB payload keeps its write control and data through a data-bus stall, so the held consumer can use them on release. The implementation follows the proposed issue schedule.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Verilator lint passed with all requested warnings enabled. All 86 cocotb tests passed. The new cases check bank isolation through a bypass and reset, both RVFI source values with two matching writers, and WB forwarding through a three-cycle store stall.\n- Selftest and three random instruction traces matched the reference ISS with ready buses and with both buses stalling. Ten CoreMark iterations took 4,527,760 bracketed cycles. All five CRCs matched and the UART reported successful validation.\n- Gowin placement options 0, 1 and 2 reached 78.911, 78.925 and 78.925 MHz. The median gives **174.31 iterations/s**, 18.16% above 147.52 iterations/s. Gowin reported 32 RAM16 cells.\n\nThe critical path differs across placements. Option 0 runs from the divider quotient to the remainder register. Options 1 and 2 run from the EX/MEM write control to the fetch PC.\n M cores/bench/rtl/core.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/forward_unit.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/reg_file.sv\n M cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py\n M cores/bench/test/test_reg_file.py\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
