{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_67","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md; git status --short --untracked-files=all'","aggregated_output":"# Loop replay\n\nTen stalled CoreMark iterations took **4,106,616 bracketed cycles**, down 9.30% from 4,527,760. Gowin measured **80.180 MHz** median, yielding **195.25 iterations/s**, up 12.01% from 174.31. Placement options 0, 1, and 2 reached 73.518, 80.180, and 80.180 MHz. Placement 0 used 3,872 logic cells and 803 registers. The 74.88 MHz acceptance threshold is sensitive to placement, with two of the three builds above it.\n\n## Implementation\n\nValid ID/EX slots write a 64 × 32 distributed instruction RAM. Four distributed-RAM tags and four 16-bit masks identify individual words by their full PC. Replacing a tag leaves only the newly written word present. A taken, aligned conditional branch can enable replay for backward displacements through 256 bytes. A ready imem word always wins. On a wait, an exact hit issues through the normal decoder, register reads, forwarding, and interlocks.\n\nThe tail includes PC bit 8 so the head and tail of a 256-byte backedge remain distinguishable. IF stores the word address, and EX supplies the aligned redirect directly from its existing target mux. This shortened the redirect path. FENCE.I clears all presence masks and replay state. Since issuing the fence already advanced IF to the fence PC plus four, the interlock holds that address and squashes the younger fetch when the fence leaves EX. An older stalled memory request keeps the fence in EX until acceptance.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Verilator lint passed with all requested warnings enabled.\n- All 91 cocotb cases passed. Directed tests cover poisoned imem data, replay across a load-use hazard and stalled stores, aliased tags, missing words, live-word priority, loop exit, the displacement boundary, and instruction replacement across FENCE.I.\n- Selftest and all three random programs matched the reference ISS with both buses ready and with both buses stalling.\n- Stalled CoreMark reached both timing markers and EBREAK. The UART reported successful validation with CRCs `e9f5`, `e714`, `1fd7`, `8e3a`, and `fcaf`.\n M cores/bench/rtl/core.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/decoder.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
