{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_7", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'ls -l cores/bench/test && cat cores/bench/core.yaml && cat cores/bench/LESSONS.md'", "aggregated_output": "total 84\n-rw-rw----+ 1 nobody nogroup  2143 Nov 19 21:01 _helpers.py\n-rw-rw----+ 1 nobody nogroup   361 Nov 19 21:01 conftest.py\n-rw-rw----+ 1 nobody nogroup  9130 Nov 19 21:01 test_alu.py\n-rw-rw----+ 1 nobody nogroup 11218 Nov 19 21:01 test_decoder.py\n-rw-rw----+ 1 nobody nogroup  2478 Nov 19 21:01 test_imm_gen.py\n-rw-rw----+ 1 nobody nogroup 43752 Nov 19 21:01 test_pipeline.py\n-rw-rw----+ 1 nobody nogroup  3525 Nov 19 21:01 test_reg_file.py\nname: bench\ndescription: Frozen LLM-benchmark fixture core. Identical RTL to baseline at the bench-fixture-v1\n  freeze point. The benchmark matrix clones from the bench-fixture-v1 orphan branch\n  and operates only on this directory.\nisa: rv32im\nnret: 1\ntarget_fpga: tang-nano-20k\ntargets:\n  fmax_mhz: 135\n  lut4: 10000\n  coremark_iter_s: 300\n  coremark_per_mhz: 2.2\ncurrent:\n  fmax_mhz: 83.741\n  lut4: 3609\n  ff: 995\n  coremark_iter_s: 3.0e-06\n  coremark_per_mhz: 0.0\n  source_id: hyp-20261119-003-r9s2\n  updated: '2026-11-19T19:41:16.779474Z'\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-001-r1s0 (regression, +476.16%): CoreMark executes no divide or remainder between its timing markers, so chaining eight restoring steps per cycle saved no benchmark cycles over six and lowered median Gowin Fmax from 43.1 to 31.4 MHz.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-002-r1s1 (improvement, +692.41%): Replacing combinational DIV/REM in EX with a shared six-round restoring divider shortened the critical path and raised median Gowin Fmax from 5.44 to 43.1 MHz, while timed CoreMark incurred no divider stalls because it executed neither operation.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-003-r1s2 (regression, +7.76%): Correctly predicting a taken branch or JAL in fetch avoided a one-cycle EX redirect bubble, but the combinational DIV/REM path still limited CoreMark throughput to 13.06 iterations/s versus 96.04 with the six-round divider.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-001-r2s0 (regression, +10.42%): Replacing six serial restoring steps with three radix-4 digits per full round raised median Gowin Fmax from 43.1 to 47.6 MHz at the same CoreMark cycle\n…[5736 of 8808 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:2137b5d851e0180a]…\noreMark cycle count in paired prototypes.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-001-r9s0 (regression, +2.64%): Registering equality at load writeback let dependent BEQ/BNE instructions issue without a load-use bubble, saving 132,211 cycles over ten CoreMark iterations, but divider feedback limited median Fmax to 80.689 MHz, below the 82.23 MHz needed for acceptance.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-002-r9s1 (regression, +4.87%): Parallel block-RAM probes at ID/EX, EX/MEM and MEM/WB avoided the load-use bubble on validated hits and saved 285,599 cycles over ten CoreMark iterations, but divider feedback limited median Fmax to 78.681 MHz and throughput to 245.13 iterations/s versus the round winner's 245.28.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-003-r9s2 (improvement, +4.94%): Storing the instruction and predicted target in one PC-tagged row let fetch serve hits during instruction-bus waits and let EX validate a predicted-taken branch or JAL target with the same_word bit because the matching PC and instruction uniquely determine that target.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
