block-RAM BTB during fetch cut CoreMark cycles by about 7%, but the six-round divider still set Fmax, leaving throughput at 102.47 iterations/s versus 109.64 with the radix-8 divider.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-001-r3s0 (improvement, +16.18%): Clearing only ID/EX valid on a bubble let load-use detection read the\n…[5822 of 8894 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:0ebfebf34a16d98e]…\nme_word bit because the matching PC and instruction uniquely determine that target.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-001-r10s0 (regression, +6.81%): Applying the same ROM reciprocal to the dividend and then its residual let registered DSP products replace the serial radix-8 trials, raising CoreMark throughput to 261.99 iterations/s at the same cycle count.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-002-r10s1 (improvement, +8.83%): Feeding WB results to dependent ADD/ADDI, BEQ/BNE and store-data logic in MEM avoided load-use and multiply-use bubbles, while letting the next load add a nonzero offset to a forwarded aligned LW result saved another 30,917 cycles over ten CoreMark iterations in the prototype.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-003-r10s2 (regression, +7.07%): Predicting the RAM row from a matching EX load’s unverified data bits [10:2] or a forwarded EX/MEM base let 96.9% of immediate load consumers avoid a load-use stall, up from 34.2% with the decode base alone, saving 174,829 cycles over ten CoreMark iterations in the prototype.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
