 below 2^17, two radix-8 stages need only 20-bit trial operands, while at or above 2^17 the top 17 dividend bits already form a valid remainder, so one full-width stage per cycle suffices and the prototype's critical path shifted from divider feedback to writeback through EX to the fetch PC.\n20:- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-001-r8s0 (improvement, +8.28%): Packing each PC tag with its instruction in a 512-row block RAM limited a conflicting replay fill to one eviction, while parallel reads at the registered normal and recovery PCs kept the recovery mux off both RAM address paths.\n22:- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-003-r8s2 (regression, +6.78%): Comparing EX rd with rs1 and rs2 of the bus, successor and replay words in parallel with the successor PC check kept the instruction mux off the load-use hazard path, raising median Fmax from 65.969 to 77.982 MHz at the same CoreMark cycle count in paired prototypes.\n29:- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-001-r11s0 (regression, +7.19%): A piecewise-linear reciprocal kept the quotient estimate within one of the exact quotient, so one residual correction sufficed and the divider could register the corrected magnitude before the EX result mux, shifting the prototype’s critical path to WB forwarding into EX/MEM.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
