{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_85","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python3 -B -c 'fitness=5.478e6*10/4193225\nprint(f\\\"Fitness {fitness:.6f}, throughput change {(fitness/12.12-1):.4%}, IPC change {(4491485/4193225-1):.4%}\\\")' && git diff --check && cat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md && git status --short\"","aggregated_output":"Fitness 13.063931, throughput change 7.7882%, IPC change 7.1129%\n# Direct control-transfer prediction\n\nFetch indexes 256 two-bit saturating counters with PC[9:2]. The counter RAM has an asynchronous read and a synchronous EX write. Reset clears the valid bitmap. A cold entry supplies 10 for a negative displacement and 01 otherwise. Fetch predicts legal conditional branches from the counter MSB and predicts JAL taken when the direct target is word-aligned and within the first MiB.\n\nThe ID/EX register carries the selected direction and counter snapshot through stalls. EX resolves the instruction using forwarded operands. A direction miss flushes the younger fetch and selects the resolved target or fall-through. A held branch trains when the older data access completes. RVFI receives the resolved successor, including PC+4 for a misalignment trap.\n\nThe existing hazard unit already holds fetch until ID can accept an instruction and bubbles ID on recovery. Its redirect input now receives the qualified prediction miss. The interface follows the bench core's declared single retirement channel.\n\nBranches separated by 1 KiB share a counter. A same-index lookup concurrent with training captures the pre-write state. Either can affect prediction accuracy. EX checks the direction for each instruction independently.\n M cores/bench/rtl/core.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/core_pkg.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/id_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
