{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_38","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/LESSONS.md; git diff --stat; git diff --check'","aggregated_output":"- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-001-r1s0 (regression, +476.16%): CoreMark executes no divide or remainder between its timing markers, so chaining eight restoring steps per cycle saved no benchmark cycles over six and lowered median Gowin Fmax from 43.1 to 31.4 MHz.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-002-r1s1 (improvement, +692.41%): Replacing combinational DIV/REM in EX with a shared six-round restoring divider shortened the critical path and raised median Gowin Fmax from 5.44 to 43.1 MHz, while timed CoreMark incurred no divider stalls because it executed neither operation.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-003-r1s2 (regression, +7.76%): Correctly predicting a taken branch or JAL in fetch avoided a one-cycle EX redirect bubble, but the combinational DIV/REM path still limited CoreMark throughput to 13.06 iterations/s versus 96.04 with the six-round divider.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-001-r2s0 (regression, +10.42%): Replacing six serial restoring steps with three radix-4 digits per full round raised median Gowin Fmax from 43.1 to 47.6 MHz at the same CoreMark cycle count and shifted the critical path from remainder_q feedback to the register-file read through EX to data memory.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-002-r2s1 (improvement, +14.16%): Registering 3D, 5D and 7D during the two-bit opening round let each later round resolve six quotient bits in two radix-8 stages of parallel trial subtraction and selection, raising median Gowin Fmax from 43.1 to 49.2 MHz at the same CoreMark cycle count.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-003-r2s2 (regression, +6.70%): Looking up branch, JAL and JALR targets in a 256-entry block-RAM BTB during fetch cut CoreMark cycles by about 7%, but the six-round divider still set Fmax, leaving throughput at 102.47 iterations/s versus 109.64 with the radix-8 divider.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-001-r3s0 (improvement, +16.18%): Clearing only ID/EX valid on a bubble let load-use detection read the raw fetch word in parallel with EX redirect, while registering the JALR adder's sum for loads and stores bypassed the ALU result mux on the memory address path.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-002-r3s1 (regression, +10.37%): Fetching the resolved branch or jump target in EX let a ready target enter ID/EX without a bubble, cutting CoreMark cycles by 7.91%, but the register-file path through EX to data memory limited median Fmax to 50.052 MHz and throughput to 121.01 iterations/s versus the champion's 127.38.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-003-r3s2 (regression, +10.89%): Counting agreement with the displacement sign lets taken backward branches and untaken forward branches reinforce the same counter, so 64 entries preserved the 256-entry predictor’s roughly 7% CoreMark cycle savings, though throughput trailed the round winner at 121.58 versus 127.38 iterations/s.\n cores/bench/rtl/core.sv           | 11 ++++-\n cores/bench/rtl/core_pkg.sv       |  7 ++++\n cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv       | 55 +++++++++++++-----------\n cores/bench/rtl/id_stage.sv       |  8 +++-\n cores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv       | 55 +++++++++++++++++++++++-\n cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py | 88 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-\n 6 files changed, 194 insertions(+), 30 deletions(-)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
