{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_10", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/LESSONS.md | head -120 && cat /sys/fs/cgroup/cpu.max && cat /sys/fs/cgroup/memory.max && df -h . && ls -l bench/programs/coremark'", "aggregated_output": "- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-001-r1s0 (regression, +476.16%): CoreMark executes no divide or remainder between its timing markers, so chaining eight restoring steps per cycle saved no benchmark cycles over six and lowered median Gowin Fmax from 43.1 to 31.4 MHz.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-002-r1s1 (improvement, +692.41%): Replacing combinational DIV/REM in EX with a shared six-round restoring divider shortened the critical path and raised median Gowin Fmax from 5.44 to 43.1 MHz, while timed CoreMark incurred no divider stalls because it executed neither operation.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-003-r1s2 (regression, +7.76%): Correctly predicting a taken branch or JAL in fetch avoided a one-cycle EX redirect bubble, but the combinational DIV/REM path still limited CoreMark throughput to 13.06 iterations/s versus 96.04 with the six-round divider.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-001-r2s0 (regression, +10.42%): Replacing six serial restoring steps with three radix-4 digits per full round raised median Gowin Fmax from 43.1 to 47.6 MHz at the same CoreMark cycle count and shifted the critical path from remainder_q feedback to the register-file read through EX to data memory.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-002-r2s1 (improvement, +14.16%): Registering 3D, 5D and 7D during the two-bit opening round let each later round resolve six quotient bits in two radix-8 stages of parallel trial subtraction and selection, raising median Gowin Fmax from 43.1 to 49.2 MHz at the same CoreMark cycle count.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-003-r2s2 (regression, +6.70%): Looking up branch, JAL and JALR targets in a 256-entry block-RAM BTB during fetch cut CoreMark cycles by about 7%, but the six-round divider still set Fmax, leaving throughput at 102.47 iterations/s versus 109.64 with the radix-8 divider.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-001-r3s0 (improvement, +16.18%): Clearing only ID/EX valid on a bubble let load-use detection read the raw fetch word in parallel with EX redirect, while registering the JALR adder's sum for loads and stores bypassed the ALU result mux on \n…[1095 of 4167 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:427261cb8466b3d4]…\nouting fetch PC+4 through ALU_LUI for JAL/JALR removed the jump-link mux from EX/MEM.alu_result and raised median Fmax by 5.24 MHz at the same CoreMark cycle count in paired page-local predictor prototypes, though the final build trailed the MEM-stage multiplier at 140.01 versus 147.52 iterations/s.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-001-r5s0 (regression, +15.31%): Capturing distributed-RAM operands and forwarding tag matches at ID/EX removed the block-RAM read delay and five-bit tag comparisons from the EX operand path, raising CoreMark throughput from 147.52 to 170.10 iterations/s at the same cycle count.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-003-r5s2 (regression, +13.67%): Registering the predicted and recovery PCs in parallel let the late EX branch comparison drive a one-bit fetch selector, raising median Fmax from 61.4 to 71.6 MHz at the same CoreMark cycle count in paired prototypes, though final throughput trailed the round winner at 167.69 versus 174.31 iterations/s.\ncat: /sys/fs/cgroup/cpu.max: No such file or directory\n", "exit_code": 1, "status": "failed"}}
