{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_127","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'git diff --check && git status --short && cat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md'","aggregated_output":" M cores/bench/rtl/core.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/core_pkg.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/decoder.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/id_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py\n# Registered branch successors and 16-entry agree predictor\n\nFetch registers the full fall-through address, B/J target, and predicted direction together. A legal JAL predicts taken. A legal conditional branch predicts according to a two-bit agreement counter and the displacement sign. Fetch enables either taken prediction only for a word-aligned destination in the 1 MiB instruction RAM.\n\nThe table uses one distributed RAM indexed by PC[5:2] XOR PC[9:6]. Its write port initializes one row per cycle after reset. Fetch uses weak agreement throughout the sweep. Each advancing, nontrapping conditional branch supplies one registered training event containing its index, counter snapshot, and resolved agreement.\n\nEX resolves branches using forwarded operands. The alternate direct address is ready during the comparison, and the recovery register captures it on the miss edge. For JALR it captures the forwarded base plus displacement with bit zero cleared. A miss selects recovery during an older dmem wait. The held branch then squashes the younger slot when the wait ends. RVFI reports fall-through on a misalignment trap and the link write is suppressed.\n\nThe implementation uses the decoder's existing `ALU_LUI` pass-through operation to deliver the saved fall-through address for JAL and JALR. This removes the jump-link mux after the ALU. The existing hazard unit supplies the required stall and bubble priority when driven by the prediction miss.\n\n## Local measurements\n\n| Measurement | Result |\n| --- | --- |\n| Ten CoreMark iterations with both stall generators | 4,246,109 bracketed cycles |\n| Start and stop marker cycles | 15,157 and 4,261,266 |\n| Gowin placement options 0, 1, 2 | 79.423, 82.027, 82.027 MHz |\n| Median throughput | 193.18 iterations/s, up 10.82% from 174.31 |\n| Option 0 area | 3,413 logic cells, 789 registers, 33 LUT-RAMs |\n\nThe cycle count matches the scratch prototype. The local median Fmax is 2.226 MHz lower. The worst reported setup path for option 1 runs through the divider remainder feedback.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Verilator passed the requested lint command with zero warnings.\n- All 91 cocotb cases passed. Directed tests cover consecutive JAL links, saturating confidence across aliased branches, wrong-path store strobes on loop exit, imem backpressure on dmem release, RAM bounds, misaligned transfers, and a dependent JALR.\n- All eight ISS retirement-trace comparisons passed across selftest and three random programs, with and without memory stalls.\n- CoreMark reached EBREAK with zero out-of-bounds accesses and printed `Correct operation validated.`. The seed, list, matrix, state, and final CRCs were `e9f5`, `e714`, `1fd7`, `8e3a`, and `fcaf`.\n- Standard single-channel ALTOPS formal passed all 53 checks on the delivered RTL.\n- All 53 checks also passed on a scratch copy with the reset sweep disabled. This exercised prediction from arbitrary initial counter contents starting with the first fetch.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
