{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_113","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'git diff --check; git status --short --untracked-files=all; cat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md'","aggregated_output":" M cores/bench/rtl/core.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/core_pkg.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/forward_unit.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/id_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/mem_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/wb_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py\n# Unified instruction and successor predictor\n\nTen CoreMark iterations took **3,414,124 bracketed cycles**. Gowin placement options 0, 1 and 2 reached **81.360, 83.741 and 83.741 MHz**. The median gives **245.28 iterations/s**, clearing the requested timing threshold by 0.268 MHz.\n\nThe timed wrapper used 3,609 logic cells, 995 registers, 11 block RAMs and two DSPs.\n\n## Implementation\n\nOne 1,024-entry direct-mapped table stores the source tag, raw instruction, valid bit, two-bit counter and aligned low-memory target. Each 61-bit row spans four block-RAM slices with a common write address and enable. Normal fetch and taken recovery have independent read addresses. A ready bus supplies the live instruction, and either read port can supply an exact cached word during a wait. Only normal fetch predicts a taken successor.\n\nTraining captures each valid low-memory instruction on the edge it leaves EX, including the completion edge of a divide. Reset and FENCE.I cancel outstanding fills and start a 1,024-clock invalidation sweep. Live instructions can still issue and retire during the sweep.\n\nEX verifies direct predictions using the instruction equality bit from fetch. A zero-offset JALR compares the full base word address from each forwarding candidate against the saved target. The forwarding selects choose the matching bit. Actual target formation and alignment checking use the architectural immediate and operands. Decode supplies fetch PC+4 to the ALU for jump links.\n\n## Timing refinements\n\nGowin also identified paths through conditional branch resolution and the load-use interlock. The branch comparator evaluates the two 16-bit halves in parallel. Decode registers forwarding matches with each producer's write permission, and MEM registers the selected writeback value while retaining both candidates for the JALR comparison. Fetch checks load-use dependencies on each instruction source before selecting the match.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Verilator passed the requested lint command with zero warnings.\n- The local ALTOPS formal gate passed all 53 checks using the single-retirement wrapper selected by `core.yaml`.\n- All 117 cocotb cases passed, including 47 pipeline cases covering target recovery, aliases, modified instructions, bypass selection, alignment faults, store handshakes and FENCE.I.\n- Selftest and all three random programs matched the ISS with ready buses and with instruction and data stalls.\n- CoreMark recorded start and stop at cycles 12,445 and 3,426,569. The simulator reported no out-of-bounds access. UART reported all five canonical CRCs and `Correct operation validated.`\n\nAn initial formal run stalled while Bitwuzla parsed the monolithic 61-bit masked RAM write. Splitting the physical write into narrow slices kept the row atomic and allowed the unmodified gate to complete. The bounded formal depth is shorter than the invalidation sweep, so the pipeline tests explicitly warm the predictor to exercise trained rows.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
