ming markers, so chaining eight restoring steps per cycle saved no benchmark cycles over six and lowered median Gowin Fmax from 43.1 to 31.4 MHz.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-002-r1s1 (improvement, +692.41%): Replacing combinational DIV/REM in EX with a shared six-round restoring divider shortened the critical path an\n…[10257 of 13329 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:520b8b8b9e084e66]…\n20 hyp-20261119-001-r13s0 (regression, +3.28%): Squaring the reciprocal error alongside the first Goldschmidt quotient update let a 995-word seed ROM and three guard bits keep the quotient at most one low, leaving the path from the EX/MEM data address to fetch RAM to limit prototype median Fmax to 90.414 MHz against a 91.419 MHz target.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261119-002-r13s1 (regression, +2.42%): Bounding the quotient shortfall below 512 let one radix-8 unit correct a multiple-of-64 estimate in three clocks, but the path from the registered data address to fetch RAM limited the all-exponent prototype to 89.556 MHz on Gowin placement option 1, below the 91.42 MHz needed at the unchanged CoreMark cycle count.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261119-003-r13s2 (regression, +0.14%): Matching backedge phase to a learned exit phase let the fetch row anticipate fixed-trip loop exits, saving 14,906 cycles over ten stalled CoreMark iterations, but divider feedback limited median Fmax to 87.09 MHz against a 90.97 MHz acceptance target.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
