{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_9", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg -n -i 'divid|radix|reciprocal|DSP|timing' cores/bench/LESSONS.md | tail -35\"", "aggregated_output": "1:- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-001-r1s0 (regression, +476.16%): CoreMark executes no divide or remainder between its timing markers, so chaining eight restoring steps per cycle saved no benchmark cycles over six and lowered median Gowin Fmax from 43.1 to 31.4 MHz.\n2:- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-002-r1s1 (improvement, +692.41%): Replacing combinational DIV/REM in EX with a shared six-round restoring divider shortened the critical path and raised median Gowin Fmax from 5.44 to 43.1 MHz, while timed CoreMark incurred no divider stalls because it executed neither operation.\n3:- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-003-r1s2 (regression, +7.76%): Correctly predicting a taken branch or JAL in fetch avoided a one-cycle EX redirect bubble, but the combinational DIV/REM path still limited CoreMark throughput to 13.06 iterations/s versus 96.04 with the six-round divider.\n4:- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-001-r2s0 (regression, +10.42%): Replacing six serial restoring steps with three radix-4 digits per full round raised median Gowin Fmax from 43.1 to 47.6 MHz at the same CoreMark cycle count and shifted the critical path from remainder_q feedback to the register-file read through EX to data memory.\n5:- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-002-r2s1 (improvement, +14.16%): Registering 3D, 5D and 7D during the two-bit opening round let each later round resolve six quotient bits in two radix-8 stages of parallel trial subtraction and selection, raising median Gowin Fmax from 43.1 to 49.2 MHz at the same CoreMark cycle count.\n6:- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-003-r2s2 (regression, +6.70%): Looking up branch, JAL and JALR targets in a 256-entry block-RAM BTB during fetch cut CoreMark cycles by about 7%, but the six-round divider still set Fmax, leaving throughput at 102.47 iterations/s versus 109.64 with the radix-8 divider.\n17:- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-001-r7s0 (regression, +4.69%): For nonzero divisor magnitudes below 2^17, two radix-8 stages need only 20-bit trial operands, while at or above 2^17 the top 17 dividend bits already form a valid remainder, so one full-width \n…[3433 of 6505 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:e12d80424329f1c6]…\ncle count.\n37:- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261119-003-r13s2 (regression, +0.14%): Matching backedge phase to a learned exit phase let the fetch row anticipate fixed-trip loop exits, saving 14,906 cycles over ten stalled CoreMark iterations, but divider feedback limited median Fmax to 87.09 MHz against a 90.97 MHz acceptance target.\n38:- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-001-r14s0 (regression, +1.14%): Sharing the MEM multiplier for three divider products and using an 11-bit quotient estimate for the final residual let Gowin map the multiply-subtract to a MULTALU36X18, shifting the median critical path to fetch RAM through immediate decode at 88.396 MHz against the 91.42 MHz target.\n39:- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-002-r14s1 (regression, +0.40%): Truncating wide divisors to 16 bits with exponent-dependent shifts kept the radix-16 quotient estimate at most one high, so one multiply-back correction sufficed, but the path from writeback through EX to fetch target selection limited median Fmax to 87.749 MHz against the 91.42 MHz target.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
