{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_133","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md; git diff --numstat'","aggregated_output":"# Shared quotient-digit stage\n\nGowin measured **92.044 MHz median** across placements at 83.674, 92.044 and 92.044 MHz. Ten CoreMark iterations took **3,033,815 bracketed cycles**, yielding **303.39 iterations/s**. The median placement's worst setup path runs from WB result forwarding through EX to the fetch fallthrough recovery register. The design uses 4,810 logic cells, 1,299 registers, 24 block RAMs and 5 DSPs.\n\n## Implementation\n\n- Divisors from 4,096 through 131,071 use five radix-16 digits. Larger divisors use five radix-8 digits. Both modes share candidates 0 through 7. Comparisons for 8D through 15D use 21 bits, and their selected remainders use 17 bits. A nibble stream holds pending dividend bits and accumulates quotient digits as each round consumes its input.\n- For nonzero divisors below 4,096, a synchronous reciprocal ROM feeds an unsigned multiplier. The multiply-back and subtraction use the low 13 bits because the true residual lies in `[0, 2D)`. The divider registers both corrected results, selects the requested magnitude and applies the architectural sign at transfer. Zero division takes an explicit result path.\n- ID registers the divide instruction classification to shorten the EX issue and stall path. After the divider change exposed a path from the data address to fetch RAM, the data RAM collision comparison moved to the read/write access edge. The existing row validation rejects a speculative read of the wrong row. Hardware and ALTOPS both accept consecutive requests eight clocks apart.\n\n## Checks\n\n- Verilator lint passed with `-Wall`. All 133 cocotb cases passed, including exact multiples and their predecessors at every nonzero reciprocal ROM address, digit transitions in all five rounds, range cutoffs, signed extrema and consecutive requests.\n- The selftest and all three random RVFI traces matched the Python ISS with and without bus stalls. Stalled CoreMark reached EBREAK, passed all five canonical CRC checks and reported successful validation. Its bracketed trace contained 2,922,016 retirements and zero DIV/REM instructions.\n- The default formal suite checks ALTOPS. Real divider arithmetic was checked in simulation.\n128\t89\tcores/bench/rtl/alu.sv\n5\t6\tcores/bench/rtl/core.sv\n1\t0\tcores/bench/rtl/core_pkg.sv\n1\t2\tcores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv\n1\t0\tcores/bench/rtl/id_stage.sv\n68\t10\tcores/bench/test/test_alu.py\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
