{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261119-001-r1s0.yaml'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261119-001-r1s0\ntitle: \"Compute RV32M division eight quotient bits per cycle\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  Gowin reports a 199.837 ns, 156-cell path through forwarding and the EX ALU.\n  The single-cycle signed and unsigned division and remainder operators are\n  the likely source of its depth. The canonical 2K, -O3 CoreMark ELF contains\n  five DIVU instructions, all in main outside the measured kernel. Additional\n  divide latency should therefore cost few, if any, of the 4,491,485 baseline\n  bracketed cycles.\nhypothesis: |\n  Share one unsigned restoring divider across DIV, DIVU, REM and REMU. Latch\n  forwarded operand magnitudes on issue and compute eight quotient bits per\n  cycle for four cycles. Each bit shifts in the next dividend bit and uses\n  the borrow from one trial subtraction to select the next remainder. The\n  partial remainder and quotient registers break the 32-step feedback path.\n  Only a valid divide or remainder occupies EX for extra cycles. With near\n  baseline cycle count, a median Fmax above 5.70 MHz clears the 4.6% margin.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 50\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: |\n      Replace the four real divide/remainder operators with the four-cycle\n      magnitude recurrence and a request/result handshake. Use a 33-bit trial\n      subtraction. Restore quotient sign from the operand signs and remainder\n      sign from the dividend. Preserve all-ones quotient and original dividend\n      remainder for a zero divisor, and INT_MIN quotient and zero remainder\n      for signed overflow. Run the existing ALTOPS formulas through the same\n      capture, latency and completion controls. Keep the other ALU ops combinational.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Issue once from a valid DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU when the older MEM stage can\n      advance. Capture both forwarded source values for RVFI at issue. Insert\n      an empty EX/MEM bundle while EX waits, then transfer exactly one result\n      with the held instruction metadata via the usual EX/MEM forwarding path.\n      Retain a completed result until EX/MEM accepts it and clear pending state\n      on reset.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Connect the EX divide-wait indication to the existing pipeline interlock.\n      Keep the older data-memory backpressure signal independently available to EX.\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: |\n      Hold the PC and ID/EX for the waiting divide. Allow older MEM and WB\n      instructions to drain once. An older stalled load or store takes priority\n      and retains its EX/MEM bundle and existing MEM/WB forwarding behavior.\n  - file: test/test_alu.py\n    description: |\n      Drive the divider handshake with a bounded wait. Retain every arithmetic\n      vector and add mixed-sign and full-width cases, including high-bit\n      unsigned divisors, for all four results against an integer reference.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Exercise a stalled load feeding a divide, a dependent remainder, and\n      a store consuming its result. Check source data, results, consecutive\n      retirement orders, and a single accepted store under bus backpressure.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
