he FPGA fitness (HWE Bench V2).\\n\\nV2 times designs with Gowin EDA (the FPGA vendor's own synthesis, place and\\nroute and static timing analysis) instead of Yosys + nextpnr, whose timing\\nmodel for this part misses whole classes of paths. The vendor flow i\n23:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_10\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -c \\\"python - <<'PY'\\nimport json\\nfor r in map(json.loads,open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl')):\\n if any(t in r['id'] for t in ('r7s0','r8\n…[25017 of 28089 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:3ad8559180518c77]…\nson.load(open(f))[0];print(f,r.get('fmax_mhz'),r.get('critical_paths',[{}])[0])\\nPY\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\"\n264:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_133\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -c 'cd \\\"$TMPDIR/replay-study\\\" && tail -4 timing_eqreg/gowin_p1/gw.log && python - <<'\\\"'PY'\\nimport json,glob\\nfor f in glob.glob('freq_gray*')+glob.glob('freq_binlate*')+glob.glob('freq_thermo*')+glob.glob('freq_eqreg*'):\\n r=json.load(open(f))[0];print(f,r.get('fmax_mhz'),r.get('critical_paths',[{}])[0])\\nPY\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"Running pla\n267:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_135\",\"type\":\"agent_message\",\"text\":\"Wrote and schema-validated [hyp-20261119-001-r9s0.yaml](cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261119-001-r9s0.yaml).\\n\\nThe proposal registers load-to-branch equality so a dependent BEQ or BNE can issue immediately behind its load. A scratch prototype reduced ten-iteration CoreMark cycles by **3.78%**, from 3,495,417 to 3,363,206, and matched all five CRCs.\\n\\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261119-001-r9s0.yaml cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/.agent.hyp-20261119-001-r9s0.last'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_12", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261119-001-r9s0.yaml cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/.agent.hyp-20261119-001-r9s0.last'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261119-001-r9s0\ntitle: Register load-to-branch equality alongside load writeback\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  In ten bracketed CoreMark iterations, 134,571 cycles interlocked a load\n  followed by a dependent BEQ or BNE. Both instructions need only one bit\n  of comparison result for control flow. A scratch implementation captured\n  that bit at load completion and trained the BTB on the actual outcome.\n  It took 3,363,206 cycles with both buses stalling and all five CRCs\n  correct, down from 3,495,417. At that count the acceptance threshold\n  requires a median clock above 82.23 MHz.\nhypothesis: |\n  Issue a dependent BEQ or BNE directly behind its load. While the load is\n  in MEM and the branch is in EX, compare the returned value with the\n  branch's other forwarded operand. Capture equality, the saved prediction\n  and the recovery information on the edge that accepts the load. Resolve\n  this one dependency from registers in the following cycle. A correct\n  prediction lets the younger instruction continue. A wrong prediction\n  squashes it before it can issue a request or change architectural state.\n\n  For the timing implementation, compare candidate byte and halfword lanes\n  in parallel with lane selection. Check the other operand's upper bits\n  against the required sign or zero extension. Compute the target-match\n  and alignment checks in EX and register both correction addresses.\n  This puts a register between the memory comparison and fetch recovery.\n  A direct combinational equality bypass took 3,360,915 cycles but reached\n  only 67.198 MHz on placement option 1. The clock path through recovery\n  is the main risk to the projected gain.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Exempt only a valid load followed by BEQ or BNE from the load-use\n      interlock. Wire the MEM load comparison to EX and give an older\n      registered correction priority over younger redirects, divider issue,\n      FENCE.I, predictor training and in\n…[1750 of 4822 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:e3010ba0ddcf9b7e]…\npy\n    description: |\n      Check consecutive retirement for correctly predicted load-to-BEQ/BNE\n      pairs across byte lanes, signed halfwords and words. Exercise either\n      or both sources matching, a newer trapped load shadowing an older\n      writer, changing unaccepted dmem data, and both misprediction directions.\n      Check the RVFI operands, next PC, order, misaligned taken-target trap\n      and that a squashed store or divide has no effect.\nWrote and schema-validated [hyp-20261119-001-r9s0.yaml](cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261119-001-r9s0.yaml).\n\nThe proposal registers load-to-branch equality so a dependent BEQ or BNE can issue immediately behind its load. A scratch prototype reduced ten-iteration CoreMark cycles by **3.78%**, from 3,495,417 to 3,363,206, and matched all five CRCs.\n\nAt that cycle count, acceptance requires a median Fmax above **82.23 MHz**. Timing for the proposed lane-parallel comparison remains unverified, so the projected **5% fitness gain** carries low confidence.", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
