{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261119-003-r12s2.yaml'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261119-003-r12s2\ntitle: Predict the load's 256-byte sector to read word-presence bits in decode\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The champion retired 2,922,016 instructions in 3,033,815 timed cycles. At one retirement per cycle, even eliminating every bubble would gain only 3.83% at the current clock. The data-side presence RAM currently returns its bit in MEM, where it selects the load word and the pointer forwarded to a dependent load. An isolated source-prediction prototype with late squash took 3,034,455 cycles and reached 82.499 MHz median. It preserved throughput per cycle but missed the clock target. Fetching the evidence for the prediction earlier would avoid adding a pipeline flush to this path.\nhypothesis: |\n  Split the resident 4 KiB page's 1,024 presence bits into sixteen 64-bit masks, one per 256-byte sector. Use a 64-entry last-sector predictor indexed by load PC[7:2] XOR PC[13:8]. Each entry needs four sector bits. In a replay of the champion's retired load trace, the previous sector at that index matched the full current sector on the resident page for 529,283 of 544,852 loads (97.1%), including cold starts and PC aliases. This is a trace estimate with immediate updates. Actual MEM training latency and stores can affect the usable hit rate.\n\n  Read the predicted sector mask in decode and latch it with the load. In EX, use effective-address bits [7:2] to select the presence bit and compare bits [11:8] with the predicted sector. Register the resulting local-source candidate into EX/MEM alongside the data RAM read. This moves presence lookup ahead of MEM and gives both writeback and dependent pointer address generation a registered source select. Use a banked LUT-RAM or register implementation with a synchronous single-bit set port for accepted fills and full-word stores. The 64-bit mask read needs only the predicted sector index. Keep it independent of register-file data, address addition and fetch-PC selection.\n\n  Validate the full resident page, pointer-derived row and same-word write collision before any local completion. Track page invalidation across the lifetime of each snapshot, including stalls. Within one page generation a later fill can only turn an absent bit into a present bit, so an old zero safely uses the bus. A partial store preserves presence and updates only its enabled lanes. On a wrong sector, invalid snapshot or failed validation, use the existing bus retry with the exact address and hold that source through acceptance. Train once per surviving completed RAM load. Measure first-attempt sector matches, timed cycles and median Gowin Fmax. At unchanged cycles, acceptance requires more than 91.42 MHz.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: Implement the PC-indexed sector predictor, decode-stage mask snapshot, presence updates and invalidation. Register the candidate source with the data RAM read and integrate it with existing load validation, collision handling and bus retry.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: Cover PC aliases selecting different sectors, offsets that cross a sector boundary, a store racing a captured mask, and page replacement or reset during a held pointer load. Check RVFI data and accepted bus transactions.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
