led\n  immediately over arbitrary initial table contents. Directed simulation\n  exercised trained entries after the actual initialization sequence.\nhypothesis: |\n  Index one direct-mapped branch target buffer with fetch PC[9:2]. Pack\n  valid, source tag PC[19:10], target[19:2] and a two-bit saturating counter\n  into a single 31-bit row. For an\n…[2732 of 5804 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:947c07984e92342a]…\nll && !div_wait. Supply the source PC, resolved target\n      and saturated counter update. Preserve resolved RVFI pc_next and link data.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Connect the EX training signals to fetch and feed the qualified\n      misprediction to the existing redirect and hazard inputs.\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: |\n      Remove redirect from flush_if. Keep the advancing EX recovery on\n      flush_id so the younger word cannot execute. This breaks the measured\n      redirect-to-instruction-to-load-use path through the fetch address mux.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Check forward progress during initialization, then run beyond cycle 256\n      to exercise warm entries. Check consecutive retirements on a taken loop\n      and squash a wrong-path store at loop exit. Exercise source PCs 1 KiB\n      apart, changing and misaligned JALR targets, and recovery across imem\n      and older dmem waits. Check retired PCs, link data, traps and RVFI order.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
