{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_10", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261119-003-r4s2.yaml cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/.agent.hyp-20261119-003-r4s2.last'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261119-003-r4s2\ntitle: Predict page-local direct transfers and reuse the fall-through PC for jump links\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The champion loses one fetch slot per taken transfer. A 64-entry agree\n  predictor previously saved 301,155 cycles over ten CoreMark iterations.\n  The current champion's worst reported path ends at EX/MEM.alu_result,\n  where every ALU result passes through the jump-link mux. A predictor\n  that already computes PC+4 in fetch can supply the link before EX and\n  remove that mux from the result path.\nhypothesis: |\n  Predict aligned direct transfers within the fetch PC's 8 KiB page. Add\n  displacement[12:2] to PC[12:2] and concatenate the 11-bit sum with\n  PC[31:13] and two zero bits. For a legal branch or JAL in instruction\n  RAM, the candidate is exact when displacement[1] is zero, all bits\n  above displacement[12] equal the sign, and the carry out equals the\n  sign. Predict eligible JALs taken and use a 64-entry agree predictor\n  for eligible conditional branches. Use PC+4 for all other predictions.\n\n  Carry the chosen direction and PC+4 into ID/EX. Exact direct targets\n  make a one-bit direction check sufficient for recovery. If a predicted\n  transfer falls through, the recovery address is the saved PC+4. If a\n  sequential prediction misses, the existing full-width EX target supplies\n  the address, including the forwarded, bit-zero-cleared target for JALR.\n  In ID, also select that same fall-through PC as the ALU immediate for\n  JAL and JALR and select the existing LUI pass-B operation. EX/MEM can\n  then capture the ALU output directly for every instruction.\n\n  A scratch implementation on this champion completed ten iterations in\n  4,191,292 marker-bracketed cycles with both bus stalls and all five\n  canonical CRCs. This saves 300,193 cycles (6.68%). Gowin measured\n  57.544, 60.761 and 60.761 MHz. The median gives 144.97 iterations/s,\n  13.81% above 127.38. At this cycle count a reported median of\n  55.845 MHz clears the 4.6% acceptance margin. Option 0 used 3,407 lo\n…[3342 of 6414 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:5682d2e44539eb8e]…\ntore at the predicted target. Stall an older load\n      and withhold imemReady through the exit correction. Count accepted\n      writes and check RVFI order and successors. Exercise a local JAL,\n      page crossings in both directions, a long JAL with deceptively small\n      low bits and a misaligned JAL. Check links and trap fall-through.\nThe [schema-validated predictor hypothesis](/srv/hwebench/clones/gpt-6_2-astra_xhigh-v2-rep1/cores/bench/worktrees/hyp-20261119-003-r4s2-hypgen/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261119-003-r4s2.yaml) predicts page-local branches and direct jumps with an 11-bit target adder. It reuses the fetch-stage PC+4 for jump links, removing a mux from the champion’s critical path.\n\nThe scratch prototype reached **144.97 CoreMark iterations/s**, **13.81% above** the 127.38 champion, clearing the 4.6% acceptance margin. Ten iterations took 4,191,292 bracketed cycles at a median Gowin Fmax of 60.761 MHz. All five CoreMark CRCs, 84 cocotb cases, and 53 standard formal checks passed.", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
