{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_9", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261119-003-r8s2.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261119-003-r8s2\ntitle: Predict the next instruction word from the preceding fetch PC\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The champion's branch target buffer predicts where to fetch. Even a correct\n  prediction waits for imem when its successor has fallen out of the 64-word\n  replay store. Extending replay to predicted paths already saved\n  83,953 cycles in round seven. A recurring sequence also tells us which\n  instruction word to read one clock before it is needed, including the first\n  word across a taken jump.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add a last-successor instruction predictor in parallel with the BTB. On an\n  advancing fetch, use the source word PC to read a synchronous, direct-mapped\n  1,024-entry block RAM. Each row predicts the next instruction as a pair\n  consisting of its destination word address and its 32 instruction bits.\n  Read the row while the BTB chooses the next PC. On the next cycle, a live\n  bus word wins. During a bus wait, accept the predicted instruction only if\n  the destination matches the full current word PC in instruction RAM and\n  the response is valid. The existing replay store can still serve a miss.\n  On repeated paths that fit the table, the predicted words can feed decode\n  on successive clocks even when a jump crosses between distant blocks.\n\n  Train from consecutive valid instructions advancing out of EX. The older\n  instruction supplies the table index and the younger supplies its actual\n  PC and word. Carry the predecessor across bubbles and train once per EX\n  advance. A source-index collision or changed branch outcome normally\n  produces a destination mismatch. Even if two sources reach the same\n  destination, the recorded word belongs to that exact instruction address.\n  Invalidate rows, the saved predecessor and the outstanding response on\n  reset or FENCE.I. Use a write-port sweep with prediction masked while it\n  runs, and suppress responses to same-row read/write collisions. Keep\n  fetch and BTB training running during the new table's initialization.\n\n  Resolve load-us\n…[2084 of 5156 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:d4d714f67c0c7be8]…\nom\n      ID/EX valid, EX/MEM backpressure, and divider completion. Feed the EX\n      destination register number into IF and route the selected dependency\n      comparison to the hazard unit.\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: |\n      Consume IF's selected source-register match for the live bus word,\n      predicted successor or loop word. Interlock on that match when a valid\n      load or multiply in EX will write a nonzero register.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Warm past the sweep. Withhold imem at a recurring jump target evicted\n      from the 64-word store, hold an older store for three clocks, and check\n      consecutive retirement of the jump and target when it releases. Train\n      two sources 4 KiB apart with different successors and require a bus\n      wait on a destination mismatch. Exercise a changed JALR target and a\n      misaligned JALR, then check patched code after a full FENCE.I sweep.\n      Check RVFI order, instruction words and accepted store counts.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
