1-19 hyp-20261119-001-r3s0 (improvement, +16.18%): Clearing only ID/EX valid on a bubble let load-use detection read the raw fetch word in parallel with EX redirect, while registering the JALR adder's sum for loads and stores bypassed the ALU result mux on \n…[9603 of 12675 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:8ff1762d21014ef8]…\nan Fmax to 87.09 MHz against a 90.97 MHz acceptance target.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-001-r14s0 (regression, +1.14%): Sharing the MEM multiplier for three divider products and using an 11-bit quotient estimate for the final residual let Gowin map the multiply-subtract to a MULTALU36X18, shifting the median critical path to fetch RAM through immediate decode at 88.396 MHz against the 91.42 MHz target.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-002-r14s1 (regression, +0.40%): Truncating wide divisors to 16 bits with exponent-dependent shifts kept the radix-16 quotient estimate at most one high, so one multiply-back correction sufficed, but the path from writeback through EX to fetch target selection limited median Fmax to 87.749 MHz against the 91.42 MHz target.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r14s2 (regression, +0.53%): Using the retiring load’s PC and result byte to predict later branches saved 22,551 cycles over ten CoreMark iterations in the prototype, despite the extra wrong-path fetch when MEM corrected a taken prediction.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
