{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261120-003-r15s2.yaml'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261120-003-r15s2\ntitle: \"Compute the next local-history branch forecast one visit ahead\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  Ten timed iterations retire 2,922,016 instructions in 3,033,815 cycles.\n  At one retirement per cycle the IPC ceiling is only 3.83% above today's\n  throughput, so fetch timing matters as much as branch accuracy. The\n  learned loop-exit predictor saved 14,906 cycles, but models only a run\n  of taken branches followed by an exit. A local outcome history also\n  distinguishes recurring sequences of taken and untaken paths. Each\n  branch's next local history is known when its current outcome resolves.\nhypothesis: |\n  Compute the forecast for the next visit to a conditional branch when\n  training the current visit. Keep a small PC-indexed local history and\n  a shared pattern table indexed by a hash of the branch PC and its\n  recent outcomes. Train the old context with the resolved outcome.\n  Look up the context obtained by shifting in that outcome and publish\n  its direction for the next fetch of this branch. Use the ordinary\n  bimodal direction when the pattern entry lacks confidence.\n\n  Read history during EX. For the next-context lookup, the two possible\n  outcome histories differ only in the newest bit. Read both forecasts\n  in parallel and let the actual early or deferred MEM outcome select\n  one bit. Pack this precomputed direction into the existing tagged\n  instruction row, so a normal fetch needs a single row access. Preserve\n  the independent bimodal counter for training and carry the direction\n  actually used through both early and deferred recovery. A stale forecast\n  can cost a redirect, but only the actual operands and instruction can\n  determine the retiring PC. Measure direction misses, bracketed cycles\n  and median Gowin Fmax. Even a 1% cycle reduction requires about 90.5 MHz\n  to clear the 4.6% acceptance margin.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Read the local history and paired pattern forecasts off the fetch\n      steering path. Update their history and counters once per surviving\n      resolved conditional. Initialize confidence during the existing\n      reset and FENCE.I sweep. Publish the selected direction only for\n      an aligned, representable target and preserve exact row tagging.\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: |\n      Distinguish the fetch direction from the bimodal training state in\n      prediction_t so recovery can reproduce the decision made in IF.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Supply the EX branch PC and advancement qualifier for the training\n      lookup. Hold its context in step with EX/MEM across backpressure\n      and suppress updates for annulled work.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Use the saved direction for EX recovery and for the existing deferred\n      MEM check. Keep the original bimodal counter on its training path.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Exercise a repeating mixed-direction branch with both bus waits,\n      then switch its pattern. Check learned fetch directions, recovery\n      PCs, exactly-once stores and retirement order. Include a deferred\n      load-dependent branch, colliding histories and FENCE.I.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
