{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'tail -c 3500 cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'","aggregated_output":" valid remainder. Consume four bits per clock for five clocks. For D at least 131072, start with the top seventeen dividend bits and consume three per clock for five clocks. Share the eight low digit candidates between both modes. Only the four-bit mode needs thresholds 8D through 15D, and both its trial dividend and those thresholds fit in 21 bits. Register the divisor multiples during setup. A balanced tree uses the monotonic comparisons to choose the digit and its remainder.\\n\\nFor nonzero D below 4096, a synchronous 4096-entry ROM returns floor((2^32-1)/D). The high half of its unsigned product with the dividend gives either the exact quotient or one less. Multiply the estimate back by the 12-bit D, subtract from the dividend, and increment the estimate if the residual reaches D. Register the corrected quotient and remainder before result transfer. Handle zero explicitly and apply the architectural signs after magnitude division.\\n\\nThe structural difference from r11s1 is the shared, width-limited upper half of the digit stage. Five full-width radix-16 trials per division would pay for 15 wide thresholds on every pass. Keep the existing eight-clock issue cadence for hardware and ALTOPS. The target is a median above 91.419 MHz at the champion's CoreMark cycle count.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement the three divisor ranges, synchronous small-divisor reciprocal ROM, bounded correction and shared radix-16/radix-8 feedback stage. Preserve request capture, RV32M exceptions and ALTOPS timing.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Extend the exact quotient and remainder checks around 4095/4096 and 131071/131072, every digit boundary in both recurrence modes, recipro