{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nr=list(map(json.loads,open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl')))[1]\nprint(r['hypothesis']['motivation'],r['hypothesis']['hypothesis'])\nr=list(map(json.loads,open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl')))[2]\nprint(r['hypothesis']['motivation'],r['hypothesis']['hypothesis'])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"Gowin reports a 199.837 ns, 156-cell path through forwarding and the EX ALU.\nThe single-cycle signed and unsigned division and remainder operators are\nthe likely source of its depth. The canonical 2K, -O3 CoreMark ELF contains\nfive DIVU instructions, all in main outside the measured kernel. Additional\ndivide latency should therefore cost few, if any, of the 4,491,485 baseline\nbracketed cycles.\n Share one unsigned restoring divider across DIV, DIVU, REM and REMU. Latch\nforwarded operand magnitudes on issue and compute eight quotient bits per\ncycle for four cycles. Each bit shifts in the next dividend bit and uses\nthe borrow from one trial subtraction to select the next remainder. The\npartial remainder and quotient registers break the 32-step feedback path.\nOnly a valid divide or remainder occupies EX for extra cycles. With near\nbaseline cycle count, a median Fmax above 5.70 MHz clears the 4.6% margin.\n\nAt 5.442 MHz the baseline takes 4,491,485 cycles for ten timed iterations.\nIts reported 199.837 ns path crosses forwarding and the EX ALU. The\nsingle-cycle divide and remainder operators are the likely source of that\ndepth. A reference-ISS run of the canonical 2K, -O3 binary counted\n2,922,015 instructions strictly between the timing markers. It executed\n93,960 MULs and zero DIV, DIVU, REM or REMU instructions. All five CRCs\nmatched. Division can therefore take extra cycles with no direct stall\ncost in the scored loop.\n Give DIV, DIVU, REM and REMU one shared, blocking divider. Capture the\nforwarded operands and their unsigned magnitudes at issue. Zero-extend\nthe dividend magnitude to 36 bits and process six bits per clock using\nsix unrolled radix-2 restoring steps. Register the partial remainder and\nquotient after each of the six rounds. A final cycle selects quotient or\nremainder, applies its sign and transfers it to EX/MEM. This gives eight\nEX cycles including issue, within the existing formal progress window.\nOther instructions retain their existing EX latency and forwarding paths.\n\nExpect the timed cycle count to stay near 4,491,485. At that count a\nmedian Fmax of 8.16 MHz gives about 18.2 iterations/s, a 50% gain.\nCompare Gowin median Fmax and marker-to-marker cycles separately. The\nmain timing risk is that a multiply or forwarding path limits the clock\nonce the full combinational division is removed.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
