{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_53","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261119-001-r11s0.yaml')\np.write_text('''id: hyp-20261119-001-r11s0\ntitle: Estimate the quotient once with a piecewise-linear reciprocal\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The champion's median Gowin placement has a 60-cell divider feedback\n  path. Its late forwarding completes ten CoreMark iterations in 3,182,385\n  cycles. The marked kernel executes zero DIV or REM instructions, so the\n  opportunity is clock frequency. Acceptance at this cycle count requires\n  a median above 88.86 MHz. The previous reciprocal experiment used two\n  serial quotient estimates and left its last correction on the EX result path.\nhypothesis: |\n  Interpolate a reciprocal accurate enough to estimate the entire unsigned\n  quotient with one multiply. Back-multiply once to obtain a signed residual.\n  Its sign and its comparison with the divisor select one exact correction.\n  This leaves the sixth round free to register the corrected magnitude before\n  it reaches the EX result mux. Keep the eight-cycle DIV/REM schedule.\n\n  A scratch implementation on the current champion passed the ALU suite\n  and all five stalled CoreMark CRCs in 3,182,385 bracketed cycles. Gowin\n  placements reached 89.242, 89.435 and 89.435 MHz. The median gives\n  281.03 iterations/s, 5.28% above 266.94. Its worst path runs from WB\n  through EX to the fetch recovery selector.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: |\n      Implement the following for unsigned magnitudes A and D >= 2, with\n      H = 2\"'^32. On issue capture A, D-1 and the sign and operation flags.\n      In phase 1 read ROMs indexed by j = (D-1) >> s, with the smallest s\n      for which j fits in 11 bits. Put K = j+1. For j < 1024 store floor(H/K)\n      and use scale zero. Otherwise store C=floor(4H/K) and S=floor(4H/K''^2),\n      and use scale s+2. Register scale and a 12-bit fraction f equal to the\n      top fractional bits of (K-D/2''^s), zero-filled when s is small. These\n      bits come directly from the complement of the low s bits of D-1.\n\n      In phase 2 register P = C + ((S*f) >> 12), using the exact table\n      word directly for small j. In phase 3 register M = (P >> scale)+1.\n      In phase 4 register q = high32(A*M). In phase 5 register the 33-bit\n      signed difference R = A - q*D using the low 33 product bits. In\n      phase 6 select (q-1,R+D) if R<0, (q+1,R-D) if R>=D, or (q,R)\n      otherwise. Register the requested magnitude, then apply its sign in\n      phase 7. Handle D=0 and D=1 explicitly, including signed overflow\n      and REM by zero. Preserve the existing ALTOPS branch and handshake.\n  - file: test/test_alu.py\n    description: |\n      Check all four operations against integer division around reciprocal\n      bucket boundaries, powers of two and the largest representable\n      multiples of D. Exercise all three residual cases, alternating signs\n      and back-to-back requests with changing live operands. For D >= 2\n      check -D < M*D-H <= D at bucket and fractional-bin endpoints. Within\n      each bin M is constant. This bound makes the quotient error at most\n      one for every 32-bit dividend.\n'\"''')\nPY\npython3 - <<'PY'\nimport json,jsonschema,yaml\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261119-001-r11s0.yaml')\njsonschema.validate(yaml.safe_load(p.read_text()), json.load(open('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json')))\nprint(89.435e7/3182385,(89.435e7/3182385/266.94-1)*100)\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
