{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_1", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg -n 'micro_opt' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | cut -c 1-450\"", "aggregated_output": "1:{\"id\": \"baseline-bench-c4b3e8a\", \"title\": \"Baseline retest for cores/bench\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 12.12, \"delta_pct\": 0.0, \"vs_baseline\": 0.0, \"fmax_mhz\": 5.442, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 12312, \"ff\": 460, \"seeds\": [5.14, 5.442, 5.442], \"perturbations\": [[0, 5.14, 150], [1, 5.442, 149], [2, 5.442, 149]], \"lutram\": 8, \"bsram\": 6, \"dsp\": 6, \"critical_path\": {\"from\"\n2:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261119-001-r1s0\", \"title\": \"Compute RV32M division eight quotient bits per cycle\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"Gowin reports a 199.837 ns, 156-cell path through forwarding and the EX ALU.\\nThe single-cycle signed and unsigned division and remainder operators are\\nthe likely source of its depth. The canonical 2K, -O3 CoreMark ELF contains\\nfive DIVU instructions, all in main outside the measured kernel. Additional\\ndivi\n5:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261119-001-r2s0\", \"title\": \"Halve the divider feedback depth with radix-4 quotient digits\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The champion's worst Gowin path runs from remainder_q back to remainder_q\\nthrough 186 cells. Eight serial restoring steps per clock gave 31.365 MHz,\\nwhile six gave 43.135 MHz. Both runs used 4,491,485 timed cycles for ten\\nCoreMark iterations. The measured region executes zero divides or remainders,\n8:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261119-001-r3s0\", \"title\": \"Remove the EX redirect from the ID/EX payload path\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The 49.243 MHz champion's reported 25.605 ns path starts at the register\\nfile and ends at an ID/EX PC bit, passing through EX, fetch and hazard\\nlogic. A late branch decision currently selects a NOP across the fetch\\nword. The load-use comparator reads that selected word, and the resulting\\nstall and flush sign\n11:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261119-001-r4s0\", \"title\": \"Compute multiply results from registered EX/MEM operands\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"At 57.213 MHz, the champion's median-placement setup path runs from\\nthe register-file block RAM thr\n…[2340 of 5412 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:2d59ffb9ab851423]…\n A scratch implementation captured\\nthat bit at load completion and trained the BTB on the actual outcome.\\nIt took 3,363\n29:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261119-001-r10s0\", \"title\": \"Replace serial divider trials with two reciprocal quotient estimates\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The champion's placement-0 worst path runs from quotient_q to\\nremainder_q through 72 cells. At its median placement a divider\\nfeedback path is within 0.001 ns of the worst fetch RAM path.\\nCoreMark executes no divides between its markers. A shorter divider\\npath can raise the clock for all \n32:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261119-001-r11s0\", \"title\": \"Estimate the quotient once with a piecewise-linear reciprocal\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The champion's median Gowin placement has a 60-cell divider feedback\\npath. Its late forwarding completes ten CoreMark iterations in 3,182,385\\ncycles. The marked kernel executes zero DIV or REM instructions, so the\\nopportunity is clock frequency. At this cycle count 88.86 MHz clears\\nthe acceptanc\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_2", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/LESSONS.md'", "aggregated_output": "- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-001-r1s0 (regression, +476.16%): CoreMark executes no divide or remainder between its timing markers, so chaining eight restoring steps per cycle saved no benchmark cycles over six and lowered median Gowin Fmax from 43.1 to 31.4 MHz.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-002-r1s1 (improvement, +692.41%): Replacing combinational DIV/REM in EX with a shared six-round restoring divider shortened the critical path and raised median Gowin Fmax from 5.44 to 43.1 MHz, while timed CoreMark incurred no divider stalls because it executed neither operation.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-003-r1s2 (regression, +7.76%): Correctly predicting a taken branch or JAL in fetch avoided a one-cycle EX redirect bubble, but the combinational DIV/REM path still limited CoreMark throughput to 13.06 iterations/s versus 96.04 with the six-round divider.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-001-r2s0 (regression, +10.42%): Replacing six serial restoring steps with three radix-4 digits per full round raised median Gowin Fmax from 43.1 to 47.6 MHz at the same CoreMark cycle count and shifted the critical path from remainder_q feedback to the register-file read through EX to data memory.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-002-r2s1 (improvement, +14.16%): Registering 3D, 5D and 7D during the two-bit opening round let each later round resolve six quotient bits in two radix-8 stages of parallel trial subtraction and selection, raising median Gowin Fmax from 43.1 to 49.2 MHz at the same CoreMark cycle count.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-003-r2s2 (regression, +6.70%): Looking up branch, JAL and JALR targets in a 256-entry block-RAM BTB during fetch cut CoreMark cycles by about 7%, but the six-round divider still set Fmax, leaving throughput at 102.47 iterations/s versus 109.64 with the radix-8 divider.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-001-r3s0 (improvement, +16.18%): Clearing only ID/EX valid on a bubble let load-use detection read the raw fetch word in parallel with EX redirect, while registering the JALR adder's sum for loads and stores bypassed the ALU result mux on \n…[6648 of 9720 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:11f01902fc8b2152]…\n74,829 cycles over ten CoreMark iterations in the prototype.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-001-r11s0 (regression, +7.19%): A piecewise-linear reciprocal kept the quotient estimate within one of the exact quotient, so one residual correction sufficed and the divider could register the corrected magnitude before the EX result mux, shifting the prototype’s critical path to WB forwarding into EX/MEM.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-002-r11s1 (regression, +7.10%): Five radix-16 rounds for divisor magnitudes ≥4096 and a reciprocal lookup for small divisors eliminated the chained radix-8 trials, while using separate multipliers for the quotient estimate and residual product raised prototype median Gowin Fmax from 81.1 to 89.9 MHz for one extra DSP.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-003-r11s2 (improvement, +7.92%): With 99% of CoreMark load-wait cycles on one 4 KiB page, per-word presence bits let a buffered pointer start the dependent lookup while the core checked a shared page tag, so verified hits could complete during bus stalls.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
