{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_15", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nrs=list(map(json.loads,open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl')))\nfor r in rs[-6:]:\n print(r['id'],r['motivation'],r['hypothesis']['hypothesis'][:3600])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261119-001-r10s0 The champion's placement-0 worst path runs from quotient_q to\nremainder_q through 72 cells. At its median placement a divider\nfeedback path is within 0.001 ns of the worst fetch RAM path.\nCoreMark executes no divides between its markers. A shorter divider\npath can raise the clock for all 3,414,124 measured cycles. At that\ncycle count the acceptance threshold is 87.594 MHz.\n Estimate an unsigned quotient twice with the same conservative\nreciprocal, first on the dividend and then on the residual. Two\nregistered DSP products per estimate replace the pair of serial\nradix-8 trial banks. A 2,048-word reciprocal ROM bounds the error\ntightly enough that one final comparison gives the exact quotient\nand remainder. Keep the eight-cycle EX handshake.\n\nLet H = 2^32 and let A and D be the operand magnitudes. For D >= 2,\nchoose the smallest shift s such that j = (D-1) >> s fits in 11 bits.\nRead floor(H/(j+1)) from ROM and shift the word right by s to obtain M.\nThis equals floor(H/U) for U = (j+1) << s, the upper endpoint of D's\nbucket. Each estimate q = high32(x*M) is a lower bound on floor(x/D).\nApply it to x=A, subtract q0*D, then apply it to that residual and\nsubtract q1*D. If the final residual is at least D, subtract D once\nand increment q0+q1. Apply the architectural sign to the selected result.\n\nA scratch implementation reached 86.266, 92.151 and 92.151 MHz across\nGowin's three placement options. Stalled CoreMark passed all five CRCs\nin 3,414,124 bracketed cycles. The median gives 269.91 iterations/s,\n10.04% above the champion. The design used 15 BSRAMs and six DSPs,\nup from 11 and two. The median placement's worst path now runs from\nwriteback to the EX/MEM ALU result. The ALU and pipeline suites and\ndirected reciprocal-boundary tests passed on the scratch RTL.\n\nhyp-20261119-002-r10s1 The champion spends 3,414,124 cycles on ten CoreMark iterations. Its\ndecode interlock charges an immediate consumer a cycle even when it needs\nthe missing value only for a comparison, an addition or store data. In a\n\n…[9020 of 12092 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:c0c9bf3d129ae6b3]…\n page, row provenance, alignment and absence of a read/write collision. If verification fails, hold the older load and its consumer, latch a retry bit and obtain the word on the external bus. Keep the request stable to acceptance. A verified local load advances while the bus is unready. The same completing raw word feeds the pointer handoff, load formatting and RVFI.\n\nA scratch prototype took 3,033,815 bracketed cycles for ten iterations and matched all five CRCs. Three Gowin placements reached 82.715, 83.077 and 83.077 MHz for 273.84 iterations/s, 2.58% above the champion. Starting with an empty page selector preserved the cycle count and passed all 59 pipeline tests. Its three placements reached 79.201, 80.966 and 80.966 MHz, yielding 266.88 iterations/s. Acceptance at that cycle count requires more than 84.710 MHz. Keep the 12-bit speculative index adder separate from the three architectural address candidates. Sharing its inputs with the full-width sums reduced median Fmax to 81.171 MHz in a paired run.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
