{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_12", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor r in map(json.loads,open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl')):\n if r['id'].endswith(('r10s0','r11s0','r11s1')):\n  print(r['id'],r['hypothesis']['hypothesis'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261119-001-r10s0 Estimate an unsigned quotient twice with the same conservative\nreciprocal, first on the dividend and then on the residual. Two\nregistered DSP products per estimate replace the pair of serial\nradix-8 trial banks. A 2,048-word reciprocal ROM bounds the error\ntightly enough that one final comparison gives the exact quotient\nand remainder. Keep the eight-cycle EX handshake.\n\nLet H = 2^32 and let A and D be the operand magnitudes. For D >= 2,\nchoose the smallest shift s such that j = (D-1) >> s fits in 11 bits.\nRead floor(H/(j+1)) from ROM and shift the word right by s to obtain M.\nThis equals floor(H/U) for U = (j+1) << s, the upper endpoint of D's\nbucket. Each estimate q = high32(x*M) is a lower bound on floor(x/D).\nApply it to x=A, subtract q0*D, then apply it to that residual and\nsubtract q1*D. If the final residual is at least D, subtract D once\nand increment q0+q1. Apply the architectural sign to the selected result.\n\nA scratch implementation reached 86.266, 92.151 and 92.151 MHz across\nGowin's three placement options. Stalled CoreMark passed all five CRCs\nin 3,414,124 bracketed cycles. The median gives 269.91 iterations/s,\n10.04% above the champion. The design used 15 BSRAMs and six DSPs,\nup from 11 and two. The median placement's worst path now runs from\nwriteback to the EX/MEM ALU result. The ALU and pipeline suites and\ndirected reciprocal-boundary tests passed on the scratch RTL.\n\nhyp-20261119-001-r11s0 Interpolate a reciprocal accurate enough to estimate the entire unsigned\nquotient with one multiply. Multiply the estimate by the divisor and subtract\nthe product from the dividend to obtain a signed residual.\nIts sign and its comparison with the divisor select one exact correction.\nThis leaves the sixth round free to register the corrected magnitude before\nit reaches the EX result mux. Keep the eight-cycle DIV/REM schedule.\n\nThe two products use the same unsigned 32x32 multiplier on successive\nclocks. On the current champion this scratch implementation passed the\nALU and pipeline suite\n…[1345 of 4417 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:9a0efe4593bbc6e4]…\nrocal truncation loses\nless than one quotient unit, so q is exact or one low. Form R = A - q*D.\nIf R >= D, increment q and subtract D once. Place register boundaries\nbetween the lookup, the two DSP products and correction. Both paths\ndeliver their selected signed quotient or remainder at the existing\ncompletion edge.\n\nA scratch implementation passed the ALU and pipeline suites and all\nfive CoreMark CRCs with both buses stalled. A separate sweep checked\n896,000 DIV, DIVU, REM and REMU results against integer arithmetic,\nincluding every small divisor and both correction outcomes. CoreMark\ntook 3,182,385 bracketed cycles. Gowin placements reached 83.749,\n89.856 and 89.856 MHz, giving 282.35 iterations/s at the median,\n5.77% above the champion. At the median placement, the worst path\nran from EX/MEM.alu_op to MEM/WB.wb_data. Gowin used 4,764 logic\ncells, 19 BSRAMs and five DSPs. Sharing the two reciprocal-path\nproducts in one multiplier saved one DSP and lowered the median\nto 81.108 MHz at the same cycle count.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
