{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_15", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor r in map(json.loads,open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl')):\n if r['id'].endswith(('r11s2','r12s2')):\n  print(r['id'],r['hypothesis']['hypothesis'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261119-003-r11s2 Predict that an initialized word offset refers to the currently resident 4 KiB ordinary-RAM page. Learn the page online from repeated accesses. Keep 1024 raw words and a separate one-bit presence RAM. When an aligned LW supplies the next load's base, use the unchecked buffered pointer plus the younger displacement to launch its word and presence lookups. For other loads use the forwarded base plus displacement. Register one bit certifying that the index came from an ordinary forwarded base or from a pointer that the older load actually supplied locally. Hold this information with the load across waits.\n\nA present offset predicts a local completion in MEM. Use that source prediction to compute the dependent pointer address while independently verifying the page, row provenance, alignment and absence of a read/write collision. If verification fails, hold the older load and its consumer, latch a retry bit and obtain the word on the external bus. Keep the request stable to acceptance. A verified local load advances while the bus is unready. The same completing raw word feeds the pointer handoff, load formatting and RVFI.\n\nA scratch prototype took 3,033,815 bracketed cycles for ten iterations and matched all five CRCs. Three Gowin placements reached 82.715, 83.077 and 83.077 MHz for 273.84 iterations/s, 2.58% above the champion. Starting with an empty page selector preserved the cycle count and passed all 59 pipeline tests. Its three placements reached 79.201, 80.966 and 80.966 MHz, yielding 266.88 iterations/s. Acceptance at that cycle count requires more than 84.710 MHz. Keep the 12-bit speculative index adder separate from the three architectural address candidates. Sharing its inputs with the full-width sums reduced median Fmax to 81.171 MHz in a paired run.\n\nhyp-20261119-003-r12s2 Split the resident 4 KiB page's 1,024 presence bits into sixteen 64-bit masks, one per 256-byte sector. Use a 64-entry last-sector predictor indexed by load PC[7:2] XOR PC[13:8]. Each entry needs four sector bits. In a replay of the champion's retired load trace, the previous sector at that index matched the full current sector on the resident page for 529,283 of 544,852 loads (97.1%), including cold starts and PC aliases. This is a trace estimate with immediate updates. Actual MEM training latency and stores can affect the usable hit rate.\n\nRead the predicted sector mask in decode and latch it with the load. In EX, use effective-address bits [7:2] to select the presence bit and compare bits [11:8] with the predicted sector. Register the resulting local-source candidate into EX/MEM alongside the data RAM read. This moves presence lookup ahead of MEM and gives both writeback and dependent pointer address generation a registered source select. Use a banked LUT-RAM or register implementation with a synchronous single-bit set port for accepted fills and full-word stores. The 64-bit mask read needs only the predicted sector index. Keep it independent of register-file data, address addition and fetch-PC selection.\n\nValidate the full resident page, pointer-derived row and same-word write collision before any local completion. Track page invalidation across the lifetime of each snapshot, including stalls. Within one page generation a later fill can only turn an absent bit into a present bit, so an old zero safely uses the bus. A partial store preserves presence and updates only its enabled lanes. On a wrong sector, invalid snapshot or failed validation, use the existing bus retry with the exact address and hold that source through acceptance. Train once per surviving completed RAM load. Measure first-attempt sector matches, timed cycles and median Gowin Fmax. At unchanged cycles, acceptance requires more than 91.42 MHz.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
