{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_1", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat cores/bench/LESSONS.md; rg -n 'micro_opt|restoring' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | cut -c1-3600\"", "aggregated_output": "- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-001-r1s0 (regression, +476.16%): CoreMark executes no divide or remainder between its timing markers, so chaining eight restoring steps per cycle saved no benchmark cycles over six and lowered median Gowin Fmax from 43.1 to 31.4 MHz.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-002-r1s1 (improvement, +692.41%): Replacing combinational DIV/REM in EX with a shared six-round restoring divider shortened the critical path and raised median Gowin Fmax from 5.44 to 43.1 MHz, while timed CoreMark incurred no divider stalls because it executed neither operation.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-003-r1s2 (regression, +7.76%): Correctly predicting a taken branch or JAL in fetch avoided a one-cycle EX redirect bubble, but the combinational DIV/REM path still limited CoreMark throughput to 13.06 iterations/s versus 96.04 with the six-round divider.\n1:{\"id\": \"baseline-bench-c4b3e8a\", \"title\": \"Baseline retest for cores/bench\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 12.12, \"delta_pct\": 0.0, \"vs_baseline\": 0.0, \"fmax_mhz\": 5.442, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 12312, \"ff\": 460, \"seeds\": [5.14, 5.442, 5.442], \"perturbations\": [[0, 5.14, 150], [1, 5.442, 149], [2, 5.442, 149]], \"lutram\": 8, \"bsram\": 6, \"dsp\": 6, \"critical_path\": {\"from\": \"cpu/u_mem/reg_q.rd_3_s0\", \"to\": \"dmem_0_dmem_0_0_0_s\", \"slack_ns\": -189.542, \"arrival_ns\": 199.837, \"logic_cells\": 156, \"modules\": [\"clock_ibuf\", \"cpu/u_fwd\", \"cpu/u_ex\", \"cpu/u_ex/u_alu\", \"cpu/u_ex\"]}, \"logic_levels\": 150, \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-11-19T14:06:27.263256Z\", \"round_id\": 0, \"slot\": 0}\n2:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261119-001-r1s0\", \"title\": \"Compute RV32M division eight quotient bits per cycle\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"Gowin reports a 199.837 ns, 156-cell path through forwarding and the EX ALU.\\nThe single-cycle signed and unsigned division and remainder operators are\\nthe likely source of its depth. The canonical 2K, -O3 CoreMark ELF contai\n…[5813 of 8885 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:1333bfe4fee50cf6]…\nfor DIV by zero, and handle INT_MIN / -1 and its zero remainder.\\nUnder RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS carry the existing per-op substitute through\\nthe same issue, busy and completion sequence.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Start only a valid decoded division when the older MEM request can\\nadvance. Snapshot both forwarded source values for RVFI as well as\\narithmetic. While waiting, send empty EX/MEM entries with all\\nside-effect controls clear. On completion transfer the held instruction\\nand result exactly once through the existing EX/MEM forwarding path.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Hold PC and ID/EX from division issue through the last arithmetic round.\\nLet older EX/MEM and MEM/WB entries drain. Give an older stalled memory\\nrequest priority so its address, data and enables stay stable. Release\\nthe front end on result transfer, subject to normal fetch backpressure.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Connect the EX divider wait signal to hazard control. Keep EX\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
