{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_5", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor l in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(l)\n if 'r5s1' in l[:300] or 'r5s0' in l[:300] or 'r2s1' in l[:300]: print(str(d)[:15000])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "{'id': 'hyp-20261119-002-r2s1', 'title': 'Resolve two radix-8 quotient digits per divider clock', 'category': 'structural', 'motivation': \"Gowin traces the champion's 27.849 ns worst path from the divider remainder\\nregister back to itself. Each update chains six full-width trial subtractions\\nand six dependent selections. Eight serial steps per clock achieved only\\n31.365 MHz, compared with 43.135 MHz for six. The timed CoreMark trace\\ncontains zero DIV or REM instructions, so shortening this feedback path\\ncan improve throughput across all 4,491,485 measured cycles.\\n\", 'hypothesis': {'motivation': \"Gowin traces the champion's 27.849 ns worst path from the divider remainder\\nregister back to itself. Each update chains six full-width trial subtractions\\nand six dependent selections. Eight serial steps per clock achieved only\\n31.365 MHz, compared with 43.135 MHz for six. The timed CoreMark trace\\ncontains zero DIV or REM instructions, so shortening this feedback path\\ncan improve throughput across all 4,491,485 measured cycles.\\n\", 'hypothesis': 'Replace the six serial binary decisions with two radix-8 digit decisions\\nper clock. Keep the existing eight-cycle EX schedule. At issue, latch the\\noperand magnitudes and sign controls. In the first arithmetic round,\\nconsume the top two dividend bits with two binary steps starting from\\nzero. In parallel, form and register 3D, 5D and 7D from the registered\\ndivisor magnitude D. Five further rounds consume six bits each, covering\\nthe remaining 30 bits exactly. The final cycle selects and signs the result.\\n\\nFor each radix-8 digit, append three dividend bits to the remainder to