{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_36", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"ls '/srv/hwebench/tmp/800f6bc6/1fef7d78/r6micro.kJjowIv1/skid'/summary*; cat cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python3 -c 'import json,sys; d=next(x for l in sys.stdin if (x:=json.loads(l))[\\\"id\\\"].endswith(\\\"r3s1\\\")); print(d[\\\"hypothesis\\\"][\\\"hypothesis\\\"]); [print(c[\\\"description\\\"]) for c in d[\\\"changes\\\"]]'\"", "aggregated_output": "/srv/hwebench/tmp/800f6bc6/1fef7d78/r6micro.kJjowIv1/skid/summary012.json\nPut the resolved EX target on io_imemAddr as soon as a valid transfer is\nknown to be taken and aligned. Otherwise present the saved sequential\naddress. Pair the selected address with io_imemData at ID. A ready target\ncan enter ID/EX on the same edge that its branch advances to EX/MEM. The\naddress selection uses registered EX operands and registered bypass\nsources, so it is independent of the current imem response and bus-ready\ninputs. Back-to-back transfers each resolve their own target in EX.\n\nStore a fetch base and a one-bit increment flag. The sequential candidate\nis either the base or base+4. On reset both registers clear. On a redirect\ncapture the resolved target as the base and set the flag to whether ID\naccepted that target this cycle. This handles both a ready target and a\ntarget that needs retrying. On an ordinary accepted fetch capture its PC\nand set the flag. Otherwise hold both. Computing base+4 after the register\nkeeps a second 32-bit addition off the redirect path.\n\nAn isolated prototype completed ten canonical iterations in 4,136,096\nbracketed cycles, saving 355,389 cycles (7.91%). Gowin reported 48.789,\n49.314 and 49.314 MHz across the three placement options. The median gives\n119.23 iterations/s, 8.75% above 109.64. At this cycle count the acceptance\nthreshold requires more than 47.4342 MHz. The prototype passed all 53\nsingle-retire ALTOPS formal checks, the CoreMark CRC checks and RVFI trace\ncomparisons for selftest and all three random programs with and without\nbus stalls. Recheck cycle count and median Fmax on the implementation.\n\nReplace the next-PC register with the base and increment flag. Mux\nthe resolved target or sequential candidate onto both imem_addr and\nout.pc. Pass imem_data directly to ID, with imemReady determining\nfetch validity. Update the two registers from redirect and acceptance\nas described above, including a held EX redirect during a dmem stall.\n\nGenerate redirect from in.valid, the forwarded branch comparison or\njump control, and the existing alignment checks. Keep the target\nvisible throughout an older memory stall. Use the existing JALR bit-0\nclearing, misalignment trap and link writeback. Mask EX/MEM controls\non invalid EX entries and divider bubbles so their payloads cannot\nforward, write, redirect or start a memory access.\n\nRemove redirect from fetch invalidation and ID flushing. A valid\ntarget response now belongs to the branch's architectural successor.\nA blocked older memory operation or divider wait holds ID/EX. A\nload-use conflict bubbles ID/EX when it can advance. Hold ordinary\nfetch on any of these stalls or when imemReady is low.\n\nTrack ID/EX.valid separately from the payload. Reset clears valid.\nWhen the backend can advance, capture the raw payload and set valid\nfrom fetch validity and the load-use interlock. Hold the live entry\nacross dmem and divider stalls, including a branch awaiting advance.\n\nRoute the EX redirect to IF and remove its connection to hazard flush\nlogic. Qualify the load-use producer with ID/EX.valid and retain the\nexisting held-WB bypass for consumers waiting behind a memory stall.\n\nWith both buses ready, check that a taken branch and its target retire\non consecutive cycles. Chain a JAL to a dependent JALR and check both\nlinks. Withdraw imemReady on a resolved target and hold a branch behind\na blocked older store. Check target retry, exact channel-0 PCs and order,\nand exactly one accepted store. A misaligned target must retire a trap\nand continue at fall-through.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
