{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_42","type":"agent_message","text":"The divider prototype reached 73.451 MHz and missed the acceptance threshold. A one-word fetch buffer did better: it saved 151,989 bracketed cycles and reached a median 81.759 MHz, yielding 186.84 iterations/s. I’m revising the hypothesis around that single change and checking its behavior under stalls and redirects."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_43","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261119-001-r6s0.yaml')\np.write_text(\\\"\\\"\\\"id: hyp-20261119-001-r6s0\ntitle: Buffer one ready fetch when decode is held\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  A load-use interlock, data-memory wait or busy divider can hold decode\n  while the instruction bus presents a ready word. The champion keeps\n  the fetch PC on that word and retries it. Capturing the ready response\n  lets fetch move one word ahead and lets decode use the saved word even\n  if instruction memory stalls on the release cycle.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add one fall-through buffer for an instruction and its PC in IF. With\n  the buffer empty, decode sees the bus directly. A backend hold captures\n  one ready word into the buffer. When decode resumes, it consumes the\n  buffered word and a ready bus response can refill the slot on the same\n  edge. Reset and EX redirect discard the buffered word.\n\n  A scratch prototype ran ten stalled CoreMark iterations in 4,375,771\n  bracketed cycles, down from 4,527,760, and passed the five CRC checks.\n  Gowin placements 0, 1 and 2 reached 76.689, 81.759 and 81.759 MHz.\n  The median gives 186.84 iterations/s, 7.19% above 174.31. At this\n  cycle count the implementation needs more than 79.78 MHz to clear\n  the acceptance margin.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 7\n  confidence: high\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Keep the PC as the address of the next bus word. Add buffered_valid,\n      buffered_pc and buffered_instr. Feed out.pc and out.instr from the\n      slot when valid and directly from the bus PC and data otherwise.\n      Set out.valid to buffered_valid OR imem_ready. Let take_bus be\n      imem_ready AND (the slot is empty OR decode can consume). On a\n      take_bus edge capture pc and imem_data into the payload registers.\n      Give reset and redirect priority in the PC and valid registers,\n      setting the PC to the reset vector or redirect target respectively\n      and clearing buffered_valid. Otherwise advance PC by four on\n      take_bus. Hold a full slot while decode is blocked. On all other\n      edges set buffered_valid to imem_ready AND (buffered_valid OR stall).\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: |\n      Drive stall_if from load_use_hazard, dmem_stall and div_wait.\n      Let IF handle instruction-bus readiness locally so a buffered\n      instruction can advance while the bus waits. Retain the ID/EX\n      bubble for a load or multiply dependency and for an EX redirect.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Remove the instruction-ready input connection to the hazard unit.\n      Keep io_imemReady connected to IF. Feed decode and its source-tag\n      comparisons from the selected IF output so the interlock follows\n      the oldest fetched instruction.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Hold a dependent instruction behind LW with fetch ready, then\n      deassert imemReady at the following PC. Check the saved instruction\n      retires once with the loaded value. Exercise simultaneous drain and\n      refill, then redirect while the slot contains a wrong-path store.\n      Check ordered PCs, register data and accepted writes under bus waits.\n\\\"\\\"\\\")\nPY\ncp -r cores/bench/test '/srv/hwebench/tmp/800f6bc6/1fef7d78/r6micro.kJjowIv1/skid/'\nls '/srv/hwebench/tmp/800f6bc6/1fef7d78/r6micro.kJjowIv1/fwd'/summary*; cat formal/run_all.sh | head -170\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_43", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261119-001-r6s0.yaml')\np.write_text(\\\"\\\"\\\"id: hyp-20261119-001-r6s0\ntitle: Buffer one ready fetch when decode is held\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  A load-use interlock, data-memory wait or busy divider can hold decode\n  while the instruction bus presents a ready word. The champion keeps\n  the fetch PC on that word and retries it. Capturing the ready response\n  lets fetch move one word ahead and lets decode use the saved word even\n  if instruction memory stalls on the release cycle.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add one fall-through buffer for an instruction and its PC in IF. With\n  the buffer empty, decode sees the bus directly. A backend hold captures\n  one ready word into the buffer. When decode resumes, it consumes the\n  buffered word and a ready bus response can refill the slot on the same\n  edge. Reset and EX redirect discard the buffered word.\n\n  A scratch prototype ran ten stalled CoreMark iterations in 4,375,771\n  bracketed cycles, down from 4,527,760, and passed the five CRC checks.\n  Gowin placements 0, 1 and 2 reached 76.689, 81.759 and 81.759 MHz.\n  The median gives 186.84 iterations/s, 7.19% above 174.31. At this\n  cycle count the implementation needs more than 79.78 MHz to clear\n  the acceptance margin.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 7\n  confidence: high\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Keep the PC as the address of the next bus word. Add buffered_valid,\n      buffered_pc and buffered_instr. Feed out.pc and out.instr from the\n      slot when valid and directly from the bus PC and data otherwise.\n      Set out.valid to buffered_valid OR imem_ready. Let take_bus be\n      imem_ready AND (the slot is empty OR decode can consume). On a\n      take_bus edge capture pc and imem_data into the payload registers.\n      Give reset and redirect priority in the PC and valid registers,\n      setting the PC to the reset vector or redirect target respectively\n      and clearing buffered_valid. Otherwise advance PC by four on\n      take_bus. Hold a full slot while decode is blocked. On all other\n      edges set buffered_valid to imem_ready AND (buffered_valid OR stall).\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: |\n      Drive stall_if from load_use_hazard, dmem_stall and div_wait.\n      Let IF handle instruction-bus readiness locally so a buffered\n      instruction can advance while the bus waits. Retain the ID/EX\n      bubble for a load or multiply dependency and for an EX redirect.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Remove the instruction-ready input connection to the hazard unit.\n      Keep io_imemReady connected to IF. Feed decode and its source-tag\n      comparisons from the selected IF output so the interlock follows\n      the oldest fetched instruction.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Hold a dependent instruction behind LW with fetch ready, then\n      deassert imemReady at the following PC. Check the saved instruction\n      retires once with the loaded value. Exercise simultaneous drain and\n      refill, then redirect while the slot contains a wrong-path store.\n      Check ordered PCs, register data and accepted writes under bus waits.\n\\\"\\\"\\\")\nPY\ncp -r cores/bench/test '/srv/hwebench/tmp/800f6bc6/1fef7d78/r6micro.kJjowIv1/skid/'\nls '/srv/hwebench/tmp/800f6bc6/1fef7d78/r6micro.kJjowIv1/fwd'/summary*; cat formal/run_all.sh | head -170\"", "aggregated_output": "/srv/hwebench/tmp/800f6bc6/1fef7d78/r6micro.kJjowIv1/fwd/summary012.json\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Run riscv-formal checks against the current $RTL_DIR/*.sv.\n# Requires: formal/riscv-formal cloned (manual clone or `git submodule init`).\n#\n# Stages every $RTL_DIR/*.sv plus wrapper.sv + the chosen checks config under\n# the riscv-formal tree, then runs sby -> bitwuzla via the framework's\n# generated makefile. Tallies PASS/FAIL by inspecting each task's\n# logfile.txt.\n#\n# Usage: bash formal/run_all.sh [checks-cfg-path]\n#   default: formal/checks.cfg       (fast, ALTOPS, used by orchestrator)\n#   deep   : formal/checks-deep.cfg  (no ALTOPS, proves M-ext arithmetic)\n#\n# Env vars (orchestrator-driven, override $1 and defaults):\n#   WRAPPER     — path to wrapper SV (default formal/wrapper.sv)\n#   CHECKS_CFG  — path to checks config (default formal/checks.cfg)\n# When BOTH are unset, the script auto-detects nret from\n# cores/$CORE_NAME/core.yaml: nret=1 routes to wrapper_si.sv +\n# checks_si.cfg; nret=2 (or absent) uses the defaults. Agents\n# invoking `bash formal/run_all.sh` directly therefore get the\n# right wrapper without needing to know the env-var plumbing.\nset -e\n\nif [ -z \"${RTL_DIR:-}\" ] || [ -z \"${CORE_NAME:-}\" ]; then\n  echo \"ERROR: formal/run_all.sh requires RTL_DIR and CORE_NAME env vars.\" >&2\n  echo \"  Example: RTL_DIR=cores/v1/rtl CORE_NAME=v1 bash formal/run_all.sh formal/checks.cfg\" >&2\n  exit 2\nfi\n\nSCRIPT_DIR=\"$(cd \"$(dirname \"$0\")\" && pwd)\"\nPROJECT_ROOT=\"$(cd \"$SCRIPT_DIR/..\" && pwd)\"\nRISCV_FORMAL=\"$SCRIPT_DIR/riscv-formal\"\n# Per-invocation work dir + log, unique among every process sharing this\n# riscv-formal checkout (the K concurrent agents of a run share it through\n# their worktrees' symlink). The name used to be \"$CORE_NAME-$$\", but an\n# agent's sandboxed shell runs in its own PID namespace where $$ is 2 or 3\n# for every agent, so siblings wrote into one dir and wiped each other's\n# checks mid-SBY (V2 incident 03). mktemp gives a unique name instead.\n# FORMAL_WORK_ID pins the suffix so tools/eval/formal.p\n…[4412 of 7484 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:2a16b33221e3f65e]…\n-p \"$CORE_DIR\"\n# Clear stale .sv files first. Without this, a hypothesis that renames or\n# deletes a module in rtl/ leaves a ghost copy in $CORE_DIR from the\n# previous run, and genchecks/yosys silently picks up the old file\n# instead of the new one. CLAUDE.md explicitly grants hypotheses the\n# right to rename/delete files in rtl/, so this cleanup is required.\nrm -f \"$CORE_DIR\"/*.sv\ncp \"$PROJECT_ROOT/$RTL_DIR\"/*.sv \"$CORE_DIR/\"\ncp \"$WRAPPER\"                   \"$CORE_DIR/wrapper.sv\"\n\n# Stage checks.cfg with [verilog-files] auto-derived from actual rtl/\n# contents instead of the cfg's hardcoded list. The shipped checks.cfg\n# enumerates the V0 baseline filenames; CLAUDE.md grants hypotheses the\n# right to add new modules, split a stage into multiple files, or rename\n# files in rtl/. Hardcoding silently fails on those restructurings (or\n# elaborates a stale ghost). Strip the original [verilog-files] section\n# and rebuild from the glob, with core_pkg.sv first and wrapper.sv last.\nSTAGED_CFG=\"$CORE_DIR/checks.cfg\"\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
