{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_101", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"date -u; cat '/srv/hwebench/tmp/800f6bc6/1fef7d78/r6micro.kJjowIv1'/formal/riscv-formal/cores/bench-*/checks/reg_ch0/status; tail -5 '/srv/hwebench/tmp/800f6bc6/1fef7d78/r6micro.kJjowIv1'/formal/riscv-formal/cores/fetch-*/checks/reg_ch0/logfile.txt; cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261119-001-r6s0.yaml\"", "aggregated_output": "Thu Nov 19 16:46:07 UTC 2026\nPASS 0 261\nSBY 17:43:12 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:00  Skipping step 18..\nSBY 17:43:12 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:00  Skipping step 19..\nSBY 17:43:12 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:00  Checking assumptions in step 20..\nSBY 17:43:12 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:00  Checking assertions in step 20..\nSBY 17:44:12 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:01:00  waiting for solver (1 minute)\nid: hyp-20261119-001-r6s0\ntitle: Buffer one ready fetch when decode is held\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  A load-use interlock, data-memory wait or busy divider can hold decode\n  while the instruction bus presents a ready word. The champion keeps\n  the fetch PC on that word and retries it. Capturing the ready response\n  lets fetch move one word ahead and lets decode use the saved word even\n  if instruction memory stalls on the release cycle.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add one fall-through buffer for an instruction and its PC in IF. With\n  the buffer empty, decode sees the bus directly. A backend hold captures\n  one ready word into the buffer. When decode resumes, it consumes the\n  buffered word and a ready bus response can refill the slot on the same\n  edge. Reset and EX redirect discard the buffered word. Limit lookahead\n  to the current 4 KiB page. Issue FENCE.I directly and resume fetch once\n  it leaves EX, so an older stalled store can update instruction memory.\n\n  A scratch prototype ran ten stalled CoreMark iterations in 4,375,771\n  bracketed cycles, down from 4,527,760, and passed the five CRC checks.\n  Gowin placements 0, 1 and 2 reached 79.247, 80.896 and 80.896 MHz.\n  The median gives 184.87 iterations/s, 6.06% above 174.31. At this\n  cycle count the implementation needs more than 79.783 MHz to clear\n  the acceptance margin. Placement 0 used 3,373 logic cells and 774\n  registers, up from 3,293 and 708. All 90 cocotb cases passed. Selftest\n  and three random traces matched the ISS with ready buses and with both\n  buses stalling.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: high\nchanges:\n  - file: r\n…[1419 of 4491 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:d06cf1f6ce0249c2]…\ncode and its source-tag\n      comparisons from the selected IF output so the interlock follows\n      the oldest fetched instruction.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Update the two fetch-address expectations affected by reading one\n      word ahead. During wb_forward_behind_stalled_store the four store\n      observations have fetch addresses 16, 20, 20, 20. During the four\n      waits in multiply_forwarding_with_bus_stalls the fetch address is\n      44. Continue checking the exact data requests and retirement gaps.\n      Hold a dependent instruction behind LW with fetch ready, then\n      deassert imemReady at the following PC. Check the saved instruction\n      retires once with the loaded value. Exercise simultaneous drain and\n      refill, then redirect while the slot contains a wrong-path store.\n      Check a load-use interlock on the last word of a page and a stalled\n      store that modifies the instruction after FENCE.I. Check ordered\n      PCs, register data and accepted writes.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
