{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_1", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'ls -a; ls -la cores/bench/experiments; cat cores/bench/LESSONS.md'", "aggregated_output": ".\n..\n.agents\n.aws\n.codex\n.git\n.gitignore\nARCHITECTURE.md\nCLAUDE.md\nLICENSE\nMakefile\nNOTICE\nbench\ncores\nformal\nfpga\nschemas\nsetup.sh\ntest\ntools\ntotal 236\ndrwxrwx---+ 3 nobody nogroup   4096 Nov 19 18:10 .\ndrwxrwx---+ 5 nobody nogroup   4096 Nov 19 18:10 ..\n-rw-rw----+ 1 nobody nogroup      0 Nov 19 18:10 .gitkeep\ndrwxrwx---+ 2 nobody nogroup   4096 Nov 19 18:10 hypotheses\n-rw-rw----+ 1 nobody nogroup 141000 Nov 19 18:10 log.jsonl\n-rw-rw----+ 1 nobody nogroup  69967 Nov 19 18:10 progress.png\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-001-r1s0 (regression, +476.16%): CoreMark executes no divide or remainder between its timing markers, so chaining eight restoring steps per cycle saved no benchmark cycles over six and lowered median Gowin Fmax from 43.1 to 31.4 MHz.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-002-r1s1 (improvement, +692.41%): Replacing combinational DIV/REM in EX with a shared six-round restoring divider shortened the critical path and raised median Gowin Fmax from 5.44 to 43.1 MHz, while timed CoreMark incurred no divider stalls because it executed neither operation.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-003-r1s2 (regression, +7.76%): Correctly predicting a taken branch or JAL in fetch avoided a one-cycle EX redirect bubble, but the combinational DIV/REM path still limited CoreMark throughput to 13.06 iterations/s versus 96.04 with the six-round divider.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-001-r2s0 (regression, +10.42%): Replacing six serial restoring steps with three radix-4 digits per full round raised median Gowin Fmax from 43.1 to 47.6 MHz at the same CoreMark cycle count and shifted the critical path from remainder_q feedback to the register-file read through EX to data memory.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-002-r2s1 (improvement, +14.16%): Registering 3D, 5D and 7D during the two-bit opening round let each later round resolve six quotient bits in two radix-8 stages of parallel trial subtraction and selection, raising median Gowin Fmax from 43.1 to 49.2 MHz at the same CoreMark cycle count.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-003-r2s2 (regression, +6.70%): Looking u\n…[2320 of 5392 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:5a92327420c72c64]…\ne EX branch comparison drive a one-bit fetch selector, raising median Fmax from 61.4 to 71.6 MHz at the same CoreMark cycle count in paired prototypes, though final throughput trailed the round winner at 167.69 versus 174.31 iterations/s.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-001-r6s0 (regression, +4.92%): A one-word fall-through fetch buffer let decode consume an instruction saved during a backend hold even when imemReady was low on release, cutting stalled CoreMark cycles by 3.36% in the prototype.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-002-r6s1 (improvement, +12.01%): After a taken backward branch of up to 256 bytes, replaying issued ID/EX words on an exact fetch PC match let decode issue while imemReady was low, cutting the prototype's ten-iteration CoreMark cycle count by 9.3%.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-003-r6s2 (regression, +10.83%): In paired prototypes, shrinking the agree predictor from 64 entries to 16 let its counters fit in one Gowin LUT-RAM and raised median Fmax by 6.8%, outweighing a 0.52% increase in CoreMark cycles.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
