{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_64","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"git log --all --oneline --grep='divisor magnitude' -10\ngit log -8 --oneline --all\ncat cores/bench/test/_helpers.py | head -85\ndate -u\"","aggregated_output":"c1b3a35 log: hyp-20261119-003-r7s2 improvement\na892e03 log: hyp-20261119-002-r7s1 regression\n3ef4b95 log: hyp-20261119-001-r7s0 regression\n5de1761 hyp-20261119-003-r7s2: Use branch target buffer hits to predict and replay recurring paths (+10.6%)\n98daf6d log: hyp-20261119-003-r6s2 regression\nfc952aa log: hyp-20261119-002-r6s1 improvement\nfeb25d3 log: hyp-20261119-001-r6s0 regression\nc001d08 hyp-20261119-002-r6s1: Recirculate issued loop instructions into decode during fetch stalls (+12.0%)\n\"\"\"Shared cocotb constants and runner helpers for unit tests.\n\nConstants mirror rtl/core_pkg.sv. If a localparam there changes, change\nthe matching value here.\n\"\"\"\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport os\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\n# cocotb 2.x moved the runner from cocotb.runner to cocotb_tools.runner.\nfrom cocotb_tools.runner import get_runner\n\n# ── ALU encodings (rtl/core_pkg.sv) ────────────────────────────────────────\nALU_ADD    = 0\nALU_SUB    = 1\nALU_AND    = 2\nALU_OR     = 3\nALU_XOR    = 4\nALU_SLT    = 5\nALU_SLTU   = 6\nALU_SLL    = 7\nALU_SRL    = 8\nALU_SRA    = 9\nALU_LUI    = 10\nALU_MUL    = 11\nALU_MULH   = 12\nALU_MULHU  = 13\nALU_MULHSU = 14\nALU_DIV    = 15\nALU_DIVU   = 16\nALU_REM    = 17\nALU_REMU   = 18\n\n# ── BranchOp encodings (= funct3 of BRANCH opcode) ─────────────────────────\nBR_BEQ  = 0\nBR_BNE  = 1\nBR_BLT  = 4\nBR_BGE  = 5\nBR_BLTU = 6\nBR_BGEU = 7\n\n# ── Paths ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nREPO_ROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent\nRTL       = REPO_ROOT / \"rtl\"\nTEST_DIR  = REPO_ROOT / \"test\"\nSIM_BUILD = REPO_ROOT / \"sim_build\"\n\n\ndef run_cocotb(toplevel: str, sources: list[str], test_module: str) -> None:\n    \"\"\"Build the DUT under Verilator and run the given cocotb test_module.\n\n    `sources` is a list of bare filenames under rtl/. Order matters —\n    package files (e.g. core_pkg.sv) come first.\n    \"\"\"\n    sim = os.environ.get(\"SIM\", \"verilator\")\n    runner = get_runner(sim)\n    build_dir = SIM_BUILD / toplevel\n\n    runner.build(\n        sources=[str(RTL / s) for s in sources],\n        hdl_toplevel=toplevel,\n        build_dir=str(build_dir),\n        always=True,\n        build_args=[\"-Wall\", \"-Wno-fatal\", \"-Wno-style\"],\n    )\n    runner.test(\n        hdl_toplevel=toplevel,\n        test_module=test_module,\n        test_dir=str(TEST_DIR),\n        build_dir=str(build_dir),\n    )\nThu Nov 19 18:00:03 UTC 2026\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_65","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261119-001-r8s0.yaml <<'HYPOTHESIS'\nid: hyp-20261119-001-r8s0\ntitle: Pack issued-word replay into address-registered block RAM\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The existing 64-word replay buffer shares a tag among 16 words. Writing\n  a conflicting sector can evict instructions that have different word\n  indices. A 512-row block-RAM layout can keep more of those words resident\n  during instruction-bus waits. The existing normal and recovery PC registers\n  already provide two read addresses in time for decode.\nhypothesis: |\n  Store each issued instruction with its own PC tag in a 512-row table.\n  Read the normal and taken-recovery rows independently. Select between\n  their validated outputs with the registered recovery selector. This keeps\n  the recovery-address mux off both RAM read addresses. Retain the existing\n  short-backedge and BTB-triggered replay policy, with live bus data taking\n  priority on every ready cycle.\n\n  In a scratch implementation, ten stalled CoreMark iterations took 3,495,417\n  marker-bracketed cycles, down from 3,731,234, with all five CRCs matching.\n  Gowin placement options 0, 1 and 2 reached 75.894, 79.197 and 79.197 MHz.\n  The median gives 226.57 iterations/s, 4.96% above the champion. Placement\n  zero used 4,655 logic cells and nine block RAMs. The measured gain has\n  little headroom over the 4.6% acceptance margin.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Infer a 512x41 block-RAM table indexed by PC[10:2]. Write PC[19:11]\n      and the 32-bit issued word together for valid issue PCs in low 1 MiB\n      instruction RAM. Set one presence bit per row on fill and clear all\n      presence bits on reset or FENCE.I. Read at normal_pc[10:2] and\n      target_q[10:2], allowing RAM replication. For each port require\n      presence, a matching nine-bit tag and zero PC[31:20]. Select target\n      data and hit on use_target, normal data and hit on ordinary fetch,\n      and use the live bus on a fall-through correction. Keep the existing\n      enables and priorities for prediction, replay permission and PC holds.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Exercise two PCs 256 bytes apart that both remain resident, then\n      replace a row with a PC 2 KiB away. Check the displaced word waits\n      for the bus and an adjacent word survives. Adapt the sector-conflict\n      test to evict the exact word under the new index. Preserve checks for\n      replay termination and restart, live-bus priority, FENCE.I, ordered\n      retirement and the exact sequence of accepted stores.\nHYPOTHESIS\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
