{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_81","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261119-002-r10s1.yaml'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261119-002-r10s1\ntitle: Execute dependent integer operations one stage later\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The core inserts a bubble when a load or multiply in EX matches either\n  source field of the next fetch. The round-nine profile counted 237,530\n  such cycles in ten iterations. Deferring BEQ/BNE alone recovered 132,211\n  cycles in that round. ALU and store-data consumers can also use a result\n  after the producer has crossed the MEM/WB register. At 83.741 MHz the\n  current champion needs to save about 151,000 of its 3,414,124 cycles to\n  clear the tournament margin.\nhypothesis: |\n  Give integer consumers a second execution point in MEM. Issue a dependent\n  instruction into EX on schedule. EX resolves its available operands and\n  records which sources require the immediately preceding result. One cycle\n  later MEM substitutes the registered WB value for those sources. A small\n  integer ALU produces the result at MEM/WB, a branch comparator resolves a\n  conditional transfer, or the store formatter consumes the late rs2. The\n  producer can be a load, multiply, or an earlier deferred integer ALU\n  operation. This allows dependent ALU chains to sustain one issue per cycle.\n  Restrict deferred stores to cases with a ready address base. Interlock an\n  instruction that needs its missing operand in EX for address generation,\n  JALR or a multicycle operation. Classify dependencies by actual source use.\n\n  Carry both possible branch successors and the prediction through EX. A\n  late misprediction kills both younger slots before a memory request,\n  divider issue, or predictor update can escape. A correct prediction lets\n  the younger EX instruction advance. Give the older correction priority.\n  Use registered WB data throughout the late lane to keep the external\n  memory return off the execute and fetch paths. Measure eligible bubbles\n  removed, extra branch recovery cycles and median Gowin frequency.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: Carry deferred operation, per-source late flags, ready operand values and branch recovery metadata in EX/MEM.\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: Classify actual source reads and late-lane eligibility for the fetched word. Accept the oldest resolved correction and train a deferred branch once on its actual outcome.\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: Allow eligible consumers of a late producer to issue, retaining the interlock for consumers that require that result in EX.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: Capture available operands and tag missing ones on EX advance. Suppress early recovery for deferred branches and suppress all effects of a younger instruction killed by an older correction.\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: Substitute WB on tagged sources for integer execution, branch resolution and store-data formatting. Retain operands across backend holds. Record the repaired operands, final result, masks and actual next PC for RVFI.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: Route WB and producer readiness to both execution points. Arbitrate redirects and predictor writes by instruction age, and retain one ordered retirement on channel zero.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: Check cycle spacing and ordered RVFI for load-to-ALU chains, both sources late, dependent branches, store data held across bus waits, misaligned producers and a late branch squashing a younger divider or store.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
