{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_69","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261119-002-r11s1.yaml && date -u'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261119-002-r11s1\ntitle: Split division at 12 divisor bits to remove the serial trial path\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion's reported worst path crosses 60 cells from quotient_q to\n  remainder_q. Its small-divisor mode resolves two dependent radix-8\n  digits on that path. CoreMark executes zero DIV or REM instructions\n  between the markers, so a shorter feedback path can improve the clock\n  for all 3,182,385 bracketed cycles. A median above 88.86 MHz would\n  clear the 4.6% acceptance margin at this cycle count.\nhypothesis: |\n  Give the divider two operand-width-specific datapaths with a common\n  eight-cycle EX handshake. For unsigned divisor magnitude D >= 4096,\n  the upper 12 dividend bits already form a reduced remainder. Exactly\n  20 dividend bits remain. Consume one radix-16 digit per cycle for five\n  cycles. Each digit uses parallel trials against the fifteen registered\n  multiples of D and a balanced selection of the successful difference.\n  This removes the second dependent trial from the feedback loop.\n\n  For 2 <= D < 4096, read floor(2^32/D) from a synchronous 4096-word ROM.\n  The high half of its unsigned product with dividend magnitude A gives\n  a quotient estimate q. Since A < 2^32, reciprocal truncation loses\n  less than one quotient unit, so q is exact or one low. Form R = A - q*D.\n  If R >= D, increment q and subtract D once. Place register boundaries\n  between the lookup, the two DSP products and correction. Both paths\n  deliver their selected signed quotient or remainder at the existing\n  completion edge.\n\n  A scratch implementation passed the ALU and pipeline suites and all\n  five CoreMark CRCs with both buses stalled. The bracket measured\n  3,182,385 cycles. Gowin placements reached 83.749, 89.856 and 89.856 MHz,\n  giving 282.35 iterations/s at the median, 5.77% above the champion.\n  Gowin used 4,764 logic cells, 19 BSRAMs and five DSPs.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: |\n      Latch operand magnitudes, signs and result kind on issue. In phase 1\n      start the reciprocal lookup, register multiples 1D through 15D in\n      36 bits for the large-divisor path, and initialize its remainder to\n      A[31:20]. Register odd multiples, then obtain even multiples by\n      wiring shifts. Use a carry-save sum of three shifted inputs for\n      11D and 13D. Initialize the reciprocal ROM in four 1024-entry blocks\n      to fit Gowin's synthesis loop limit. In phases 2 through 6 bring down the five remaining nibbles\n      MSB first. Use 37-bit trial differences to retain the borrow and\n      encode the largest successful digit including zero. For the small\n      path register high32(A*reciprocal), q*D and the residual on successive\n      edges, followed by correction. Explicitly handle D=0 and D=1,\n      signed remainder and INT_MIN/-1. Preserve operand capture, busy,\n      done and the existing ALTOPS result and schedule.\n  - file: test/test_alu.py\n    description: |\n      Check all four operations on either side of 4096, including maximum\n      unsigned operands, both signs and values one below, at and above\n      multiples. Exercise all sixteen large-path digits and both outcomes\n      of the small-path correction. Alternate paths on consecutive requests\n      and retain the exact completion-edge and input-change checks.\nThu Nov 19 20:53:13 UTC 2026\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
