{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_85","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat > \"$TMPDIR/quotient/proposal.yaml\" <<'\"'EOF'\nid: hyp-20261119-002-r13s1\ntitle: Estimate a quotient prefix and restore three radix-8 digits\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion's median Gowin placement is limited by two serial radix-8 trials. Its 3,033,815 marked CoreMark cycles contain no divisions. Acceptance at this cycle count takes 91.42 MHz. Round 12 reached 90.894 MHz using reciprocal interpolation. A coarser estimate leaves enough time to reuse one digit unit and needs only a 2,048 by 23-bit coefficient ROM.\nhypothesis: |\n  Estimate the quotient in units of 64, then divide the residual by the true divisor in three clocks. This separates the DSP products from the restoring recurrence. Normalizing the dividend alongside the ROM address puts a registered operand on each multiplier input. The six low bits of the dividend pass directly to the last two trials because the estimated quotient is a multiple of 64.\n\n  For unsigned magnitudes A and D with D >= 2, let x = D-1 and e = floor(log2(x)). For e >= 11 use J = (x >> (e-11)) + 1. For smaller e use J = D << (11-e). Both cases give 2049 <= J <= 4096. Read C = floor(2\"'^34/J) from row J-2049 and register Z = A >> e. Compute P = (Z*C) >> 29. The initial quotient is 64P. Compute its residual high part as A[31:6] - P*D. The exact difference fits 26 bits and the six low residual bits equal A[5:0].\n\n  For each bucket let L be the smallest D mapped to its (e,J). C/2''^(23+e) <= 1/D. A conservative upper bound on A/D - Z*C/2''^23 is 2''^32/L - (2''^32-2''^e+1)*C/2''^(23+e). An exact rational endpoint check over all 45,055 nonempty buckets found a maximum below 257. Truncating to a multiple of 64 gives floor(A/D)-64P <= 320. Three octal digits cover that difference for every input.\n\n  Start the first trial at the 26-bit residual high part. Each trial selects the largest k in 0..7 for which kD fits its window. Append A[5:3] to the first remainder and A[2:0] to the second. Add the first digit to P while producing the second digit. Concatenating that sum with the last two digits yields the exact quotient. The third remainder is exact. Handle D=0 and D=1 explicitly and restore the architectural sign.\n\n  A full-core scratch prototype passed 61 pipeline cases, the standalone ALU suite with 3,584 additional division checks, and all five stalled CoreMark CRCs in 3,033,815 marked cycles. It reached 89.556 MHz on Gowin placement option 1. The worst path ran from the registered data address to the fetch RAM. The design used 19 BSRAMs and six DSPs.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 3\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: |\n      On issue capture magnitudes, D-1 and signed-mode flags. Read the synchronous ROM and register Z in phase 1 using fixed slices selected by the leading bit of D-1. For e<11 fill the vacated low index bits with ones when normalizing D-1. Register P in phase 2 and its residual high part in phase 3 using separate unsigned products. Precompute 35-bit divisor multiples beside the estimate. In phases 4 through 6 reuse one bank of parallel trial differences with a balanced digit and remainder selector. Register the final magnitude on phase 6. Preserve the eight-cycle handshake, reset cancellation and ALTOPS behavior. Initialize the ROM in two banks of 1,024 constants using 64-bit integer arithmetic.\n  - file: test/test_alu.py\n    description: |\n      Verify the endpoint inequality with integers. Check signed and unsigned quotient and remainder at powers of two, adjacent buckets, exact multiples, zero, signed overflow and maximal dividends. Include A=0xffffffff and D=4099, which needs a correction of 320. Retain the busy, done and input-change checks.\nEOF\ncp \"$TMPDIR/quotient/proposal.yaml\" cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261119-002-r13s1.yaml\ndate -u'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_85","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat > \"$TMPDIR/quotient/proposal.yaml\" <<'\"'EOF'\nid: hyp-20261119-002-r13s1\ntitle: Estimate a quotient prefix and restore three radix-8 digits\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion's median Gowin placement is limited by two serial radix-8 trials. Its 3,033,815 marked CoreMark cycles contain no divisions. Acceptance at this cycle count takes 91.42 MHz. Round 12 reached 90.894 MHz using reciprocal interpolation. A coarser estimate leaves enough time to reuse one digit unit and needs only a 2,048 by 23-bit coefficient ROM.\nhypothesis: |\n  Estimate the quotient in units of 64, then divide the residual by the true divisor in three clocks. This separates the DSP products from the restoring recurrence. Normalizing the dividend alongside the ROM address puts a registered operand on each multiplier input. The six low bits of the dividend pass directly to the last two trials because the estimated quotient is a multiple of 64.\n\n  For unsigned magnitudes A and D with D >= 2, let x = D-1 and e = floor(log2(x)). For e >= 11 use J = (x >> (e-11)) + 1. For smaller e use J = D << (11-e). Both cases give 2049 <= J <= 4096. Read C = floor(2\"'^34/J) from row J-2049 and register Z = A >> e. Compute P = (Z*C) >> 29. The initial quotient is 64P. Compute its residual high part as A[31:6] - P*D. The exact difference fits 26 bits and the six low residual bits equal A[5:0].\n\n  For each bucket let L be the smallest D mapped to its (e,J). C/2''^(23+e) <= 1/D. A conservative upper bound on A/D - Z*C/2''^23 is 2''^32/L - (2''^32-2''^e+1)*C/2''^(23+e). An exact rational endpoint check over all 45,055 nonempty buckets found a maximum below 257. Truncating to a multiple of 64 gives floor(A/D)-64P <= 320. Three octal digits cover that difference for every input.\n\n  Start the first trial at the 26-bit residual high part. Each trial selects the largest k in 0..7 for which kD fits its window. Append A[5:3] to the first remainder and A[2:0] to the second. Add the first digit to P while producing the second digit. Concatenating that sum with the last two digits yields the exact quotient. The third remainder is exact. Handle D=0 and D=1 explicitly and restore the architectural sign.\n\n  A full-core scratch prototype passed 61 pipeline cases, the standalone ALU suite with 3,584 additional division checks, and all five stalled CoreMark CRCs in 3,033,815 marked cycles. It reached 89.556 MHz on Gowin placement option 1. The worst path ran from the registered data address to the fetch RAM. The design used 19 BSRAMs and six DSPs.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 3\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: |\n      On issue capture magnitudes, D-1 and signed-mode flags. Read the synchronous ROM and register Z in phase 1 using fixed slices selected by the leading bit of D-1. For e<11 fill the vacated low index bits with ones when normalizing D-1. Register P in phase 2 and its residual high part in phase 3 using separate unsigned products. Precompute 35-bit divisor multiples beside the estimate. In phases 4 through 6 reuse one bank of parallel trial differences with a balanced digit and remainder selector. Register the final magnitude on phase 6. Preserve the eight-cycle handshake, reset cancellation and ALTOPS behavior. Initialize the ROM in two banks of 1,024 constants using 64-bit integer arithmetic.\n  - file: test/test_alu.py\n    description: |\n      Verify the endpoint inequality with integers. Check signed and unsigned quotient and remainder at powers of two, adjacent buckets, exact multiples, zero, signed overflow and maximal dividends. Include A=0xffffffff and D=4099, which needs a correction of 320. Retain the busy, done and input-change checks.\nEOF\ncp \"$TMPDIR/quotient/proposal.yaml\" cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261119-002-r13s1.yaml\ndate -u'","aggregated_output":"Thu Nov 19 23:10:5