{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_57", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261119-002-r3s1.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261119-002-r3s1\ntitle: Decouple fetch from decode with a one-entry fall-through buffer\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The 49.243 MHz champion takes 4,491,485 cycles for ten CoreMark iterations.\n  Its reported 25.605 ns setup path starts at the register file, crosses EX,\n  IF and hazard logic, and ends at the ID/EX PC register. Today a redirect\n  selects a NOP on the fetch bus before the load-use comparison, and a\n  blocked decode discards any ready instruction response. A fall-through\n  buffer can shorten that control path and save a fetch during a backend stall.\nhypothesis: |\n  Give IF one slot holding a fetched PC, instruction and valid bit. When empty,\n  a ready response can issue directly to ID on the same edge. If ID is blocked,\n  save that response and advance the fetch PC. When full, present the saved word\n  irrespective of imemReady. Popping it can also accept its ready successor\n  into the slot. Advance the sequential PC once per accepted response, including\n  a direct issue. Thus a fetch completed during a load-use or data-bus stall\n  can feed ID even if imemReady falls as the stall clears.\n\n  Drive decode and the load-use comparison from the raw head instruction.\n  Let an advancing EX redirect discard both the slot and the current response,\n  set the fetch PC, and clear only ID/EX validity. Keep redirect off the\n  instruction, operand and PC payload inputs to ID/EX. An older blocked\n  memory operation holds the branch in EX until the redirect can fire once.\n  Qualify stage side effects with validity so discarded words cannot execute.\n  The empty-slot bypass preserves the existing taken-branch refill latency.\n\n  A working target is 4.32 million bracketed cycles at 52 MHz, or about\n  120 iterations/s (+10%). Measure both quantities. At 4.32 million cycles\n  the 4.6% acceptance margin requires more than 49.55 MHz. The main timing\n  risk is the slot-to-decode mux on the register-file read-address path.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 10\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_s\n…[1061 of 4133 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:9953ea54dd38530b]…\n\n    description: |\n      Fire redirects only for valid aligned transfers when EX advances.\n      Suppress side-effect controls in invalid EX/MEM entries, including\n      register forwarding and memory enables. Preserve divider operand\n      capture, completion and the existing misaligned-target trap behavior.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Wire head availability, ID acceptance, imemReady and the advancing\n      redirect between IF, ID and hazard control. Qualify the EX load-use\n      producer with ID/EX.valid and retain the held WB forwarding data.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Accept a dependent instruction into the slot behind a stalled load,\n      then withdraw imemReady as data arrives. Check it issues from the slot\n      with the forwarded load result. Exercise simultaneous pop/refill and\n      a taken branch held behind a stalled memory operation with a buffered\n      wrong-path store. Check accepted writes and the exact channel-0 PC and\n      order sequence.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
