.18%): Clearing only ID/EX valid on a bubble let load-use detection read the raw fetch word in parallel with EX redirect, while registering the JALR adder's sum for loads and stores bypassed the ALU result mux on \n…[1040 of 4112 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:7a30413ef6ba8d3c]…\n026-11-19 hyp-20261119-003-r4s2 (regression, +9.92%): Routing fetch PC+4 through ALU_LUI for JAL/JALR removed the jump-link mux from EX/MEM.alu_result and raised median Fmax by 5.24 MHz at the same CoreMark cycle count in paired page-local predictor prototypes, though the final build trailed the MEM-stage multiplier at 140.01 versus 147.52 iterations/s.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-001-r5s0 (regression, +15.31%): Capturing distributed-RAM operands and forwarding tag matches at ID/EX removed the block-RAM read delay and five-bit tag comparisons from the EX operand path, raising CoreMark throughput from 147.52 to 170.10 iterations/s at the same cycle count.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-003-r5s2 (regression, +13.67%): Registering the predicted and recovery PCs in parallel let the late EX branch comparison drive a one-bit fetch selector, raising median Fmax from 61.4 to 71.6 MHz at the same CoreMark cycle count in paired prototypes, though final throughput trailed the round winner at 167.69 versus 174.31 iterations/s.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
