{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_66","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261119-002-r6s1.yaml'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261119-002-r6s1\ntitle: \"Split the divider by divisor magnitude to shorten remainder feedback\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion's reported 17.967 ns path crosses 74 cells from quotient_q[30]\n  to remainder_q[0]. Its divider cascades two full-width radix-8 digit\n  decisions per round. The measured CoreMark region executes zero DIV or REM\n  instructions, yet this feedback path limits the clock for the whole core.\nhypothesis: |\n  Use the fact that a 32-bit dividend divided by a magnitude of at least\n  65536 has at most 16 quotient bits. Give that case a single full-width\n  radix-8 step per round. For nonzero divisors below 65536, the restored\n  remainder fits in 16 bits, so two cascaded radix-8 steps need only 19-bit\n  trial operands. Compute the two recurrences independently and select the\n  completed result using the saved divisor class. This removes the pair of\n  dependent 35-bit trials while retaining the eight-cycle EX schedule.\n\n  At the champion's 4,527,760 cycles for ten iterations, a median above\n  82.56 MHz clears the acceptance margin. A median of 86 MHz would give\n  about 190 iterations/s. The main risk is that another pipeline path or\n  the routing cost of the additional digit logic limits Fmax first.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 9\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: |\n      Capture operand magnitudes, signs, and the original dividend on issue.\n      In phase 1 prepare 3D, 5D and 7D. Seed the small-divisor lane with its\n      usual two top bits, then consume six bits in each of phases 2 through 6.\n      Use 19-bit trial operands with an extra borrow bit and a 16-bit restored\n      remainder throughout this lane. For a large divisor D the top 16 bits\n      of the dividend A are already less than D. Start with A[31:15], subtract\n      D if it fits and record the first quotient bit. Feed the remaining 15\n      bits into one full-width radix-8 digit in each of the next five rounds.\n      Keep the two remainder feedback paths separate. Select quotient or\n      remainder and apply the saved sign for phase 7. Explicitly return all\n      ones for divide by zero and the original dividend for remainder by zero.\n      Preserve INT_MIN / -1 and the common request, busy and done timing in\n      real and ALTOPS builds.\n  - file: test/test_alu.py\n    description: |\n      Add signed and unsigned quotient and remainder vectors on both sides\n      of the 65536 magnitude cutoff. Include 0xffffffff / 65536, divisors\n      65535 and 65537, dividends one below and above exact multiples, and\n      consecutive requests alternating divisor classes and zero. Use the\n      existing operand-capture and exact completion-cycle assertions.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
