{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_99","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'git status --short --untracked-files=all; cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261119-002-r6s1.yaml'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261119-002-r6s1\ntitle: \"Recirculate issued loop instructions into decode during fetch stalls\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion spends 4,527,760 cycles on ten CoreMark iterations. Even\n  when a short loop repeats, every instruction requires another ready imem\n  cycle. Feeding recently issued instructions back to decode can recover\n  those slots while preserving the single-issue execution schedule.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add a loop replay input to decode. Record instruction words and PCs from\n  valid ID/EX slots. An aligned, taken backward conditional branch with a\n  displacement of up to 256 bytes enables replay and records the loop tail.\n  During the next pass, a ready bus supplies the instruction. On a bus wait,\n  a recorded word at the exact fetch PC can issue immediately. Branches still\n  resolve in EX, and every iteration reads and forwards current operands.\n\n  A scratch implementation completed ten stalled CoreMark iterations in\n  4,106,616 bracketed cycles versus 4,527,760 for the champion, with all\n  five CRCs matching. Gowin measured 72.142, 78.104 and 78.104 MHz across\n  the three placements. The median gives 190.19 iterations/s, up 9.11%\n  from 174.31. At this cycle count the acceptance threshold is 74.88 MHz.\n  Placement 0 used 4,112 logic cells. The prototype passed all 53 standard\n  single-retire formal checks, the existing cocotb suite and three directed\n  replay tests. Selftest and three random programs matched the reference ISS\n  with ready buses and with both buses stalling.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 9\n  confidence: high\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Infer one 64x32 distributed RAM addressed by PC[7:2], with asynchronous\n      read and synchronous write. Alongside it keep four PC[31:8] tags and\n      four 16-bit presence masks. PC[7:6] selects the tag and mask, and\n      PC[5:2] selects the presence bit. Write the word at the registered\n      ID/EX PC whenever ID/EX.valid is set. On tag replacement set only\n      the written word's presence bit. On reset or\n      FENCE.I clear the masks and loop-active bit. Enter replay on a\n      qualifying EX redirect. Leave on other redirects or after advancing\n      past the recorded tail slot. Keep PC redirect priority and the\n      existing interlocks. Select live bus data when imemReady is high and\n      the replay word otherwise. Compute fetch valid independently as bus\n      ready OR an active, full-tag, present-word hit.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Feed ID/EX.valid, PC and instruction to the replay write port. On\n      an EX redirect, enroll a loop when is_branch is set and all bits of\n      the sign-extended B-immediate above bit 7 are one. Save the branch\n      PC's slot index as the loop tail. Wire effective fetch validity to the\n      hazard unit and ID. For a valid FENCE.I in EX, invalidate replay, discard the\n      younger slot and restart at the fence PC plus four, respecting older\n      dmem stalls. Keep ID/EX capture, bypass comparisons and single-channel\n      retirement synchronized with the existing stall and bubble controls.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Run a counted loop with imemReady low on its second pass and poison\n      the unaccepted bus word. Check the retirement PCs, changing register\n      values, and exactly one accepted store per iteration under dmem stalls.\n      Exercise two loops with identical low PC bits and different code, a\n      cold hole after tag replacement, the untaken loop exit, and FENCE.I\n      followed by a changed instruction at a previously recorded PC.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
