{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor line in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(line)\n if any(w in str(d.get('motivation','')).lower() for w in ('load-use', 'loads', 'data-bus','load use')): print(d['id'],d['motivation'])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"hyp-20261119-001-r3s0 The 49.243 MHz champion's reported 25.605 ns path starts at the register\nfile and ends at an ID/EX PC bit, passing through EX, fetch and hazard\nlogic. A late branch decision currently selects a NOP across the fetch\nword. The load-use comparator reads that selected word, and the resulting\nstall and flush signals control the entire ID/EX register.\n\nhyp-20261119-003-r3s2 Each taken transfer costs the radix-8 champion one fetch slot. An earlier\n256-entry direction predictor cut ten CoreMark iterations from 4,491,485\nto 4,193,225 cycles. A smaller table can share a counter between a usually\ntaken backward branch and a usually untaken forward branch if it records\nagreement with their displacement signs. The champion's worst setup path\nalso crosses EX, fetch and the load-use detector on its way back to ID.\n\nhyp-20261119-001-r6s0 A load-use interlock, data-memory wait or busy divider can hold decode\nwhile the instruction bus presents a ready word. The champion keeps\nthe fetch PC on that word and retries it. Capturing the ready response\nlets fetch move one word ahead and lets decode use the saved word even\nif instruction memory stalls on the release cycle.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
