{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_49", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261119-003-r10s2.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261119-003-r10s2\ntitle: \"Predict the data RAM row a load will need before its base is ready\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The champion takes 3,414,124 cycles for ten CoreMark iterations at\n  83.741 MHz. Its fetch table already learns branch successors. A remaining\n  opportunity is the bubble behind a load whose result is needed immediately.\n  Round 9 saved 285,599 cycles on its parent by probing recent data at three\n  possible operand sources. Predicting a single row from the load PC could\n  recover those cycles with fewer RAM read ports and shorter bypass wiring.\nhypothesis: |\n  Learn the low bits of the effective word address for each recurring load.\n  Use a 256-entry PC-indexed table of predicted indices into a 512-word\n  store of recent RAM data. Start with a last-address-plus-stride predictor.\n  Train on actual byte addresses and precompute the next word index on the\n  update path so that byte and halfword streams can cross word boundaries.\n  Read the predicted index alongside fetch, then read the tagged data row\n  while EX computes the actual address with full forwarding. A PC alias\n  can select the wrong row safely because MEM checks the complete actual\n  word address and access eligibility before accepting the candidate.\n\n  Admit the dependent instruction behind the load. Capture the selected\n  lane and extended candidate in EX/MEM for ordinary EX/MEM forwarding.\n  On a verified hit the consumer completes normally and the load needs\n  no external read. On an incorrect prediction use an actual-address\n  lookup in MEM, then the external bus on a miss. Hold a dependent\n  consumer through repair and restart it with the correct operand before\n  allowing a redirect, memory request, divider start or predictor update.\n  At the champion clock, acceptance requires fewer than 3.265 million\n  bracketed cycles. Measure early hits on dependent loads, repair cycles\n  and Gowin median Fmax to test that prediction accuracy pays for the port.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: low\nchange\n…[1300 of 4372 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:aa4846eaa8773dba]…\ns\n      on reset until validity is initialized. Use actual raw data and\n      masks for RVFI, and keep MMIO on its original bus path.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Route predicted data and training between stages. Qualify recovery,\n      FENCE.I, instruction fills and divider requests with operand\n      availability. Give older memory waits priority over a younger repair.\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: |\n      Allow a load consumer to issue. Interlock on a failed prediction\n      only when the consumer needs the value. Retain the multiply-use\n      bubble and the bus handshake for external misses and stores.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Check consecutive retirement on trained load-use hits. Force a\n      wrong stride and a PC alias before a branch, store and divide.\n      Exercise an older partial store during a held predicted read and\n      check signed lanes, MMIO, misalignment, RVFI order and single\n      acceptance of every external transaction.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
