{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_99", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261119-003-r10s2.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261119-003-r10s2\ntitle: \"Follow predicted pointers into the next load before either address is verified\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  A trace of the champion's ten timed CoreMark iterations contains 544,852\n  loads. The next instruction names the load destination in a source field\n  204,039 times. A simulated 256-entry per-PC stride predictor selected the\n  correct resident word for only 53,243 of those pairs. In a pointer walk,\n  a better clue to the next address is the word just read by the preceding\n  load. That word can guide another on-chip lookup before EX verifies it.\nhypothesis: |\n  Predict the data RAM row in decode. Start with the low bits of the\n  decode-visible base register. When rs1 matches a load in EX, use the\n  low bits of that load's candidate raw word as the next base hint. For\n  a matching writer in MEM use its registered EX/MEM result, which can\n  itself be a tentative load value. Combine the hint with the displacement\n  using bitwise XOR of the word-index bits. This carry-free calculation\n  only picks a row. It can be wrong for small offsets, stale operands,\n  subword loads or pointer changes. Every load still computes its complete\n  effective address from the architecturally correct, forwarded operands.\n\n  Read one 512-entry table of recent RAM words at the ID/EX edge. Each\n  row holds a valid bit, the full RAM word address and the raw data.\n  In EX select and extend the requested lane, then register the tentative\n  result for forwarding to the consumer. In MEM an aligned ordinary-RAM\n  load may complete internally only on an exact tag match with a current\n  snapshot. A dependent instruction can execute as soon as the load hits.\n  On a miss the load uses the bus. Hold a dependent consumer, suppress\n  its effects and forward the accepted value on release. No predicted\n  address ever drives an external memory request.\n\n  In a scratch RTL comparison with an additional actual-address port\n  held constant, using only the decode base and exact displacement took\n  3,193,925 cycles\n…[1794 of 4866 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:f8b67991f85d5f3d]…\nconsumer speculatively. Hold EX and fetch on a\n      dependent miss, and retire the older load exactly once. Keep the\n      multiply-use interlock and existing store backpressure.\n  - file: rtl/forward_unit.sv\n    description: |\n      On the edge that accepts a missed load with a waiting consumer,\n      move that consumer's matching EX/MEM bypass selectors to MEM/WB.\n      Resume with ordinary youngest-writer priority.\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Save the other operand from the outgoing WB bypass if the consumer\n      waits through repair. Preserve the held instruction and its metadata.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Check consecutive retirement of warmed load-to-load-to-branch\n      chains. Change a pointer, force an XOR carry mismatch and alias two\n      full addresses onto one row. Cover both source matches, partial\n      stores during a hold, signed lanes, misalignment and MMIO. Check\n      bus transactions, RVFI operands, order and wrong-path suppression.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
