{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_103","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261119-003-r11s2.yaml'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261119-003-r11s2\ntitle: \"Predict the next data-buffer row from an unverified pointer\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  In a marker-bracketed run of the current champion, 151,744 of 3,182,385 cycles were spent waiting for reads. There were only 6,210 operand replays. The existing aligned-LW handoff already issues pointer-dependent loads back to back, so preserving that schedule matters more than eliminating another load-use bubble. At 84.949 MHz the candidate needs to save about 140,000 cycles to clear the tournament margin.\nhypothesis: |\n  Read a 1024-word buffer of recently observed RAM data one cycle ahead of MEM. For an ordinary load, index it with the low bits of the forwarded base plus the signed displacement in EX. When the base comes from the LW immediately ahead, predict that the older load hit and use its unchecked buffer word as the base for the next lookup. This lets the next block-RAM read start independently of the older tag check. Carry the predicted index with the returned row and compare the entire saved word address with the architectural effective address in MEM.\n\n  An exact, valid ordinary-RAM hit supplies the raw word locally and suppresses the external read request. A mismatch issues the architectural address on the ordinary bus and holds the request through acceptance. In either case the existing pointer handoff computes the architectural address from the word that completes the older load. An incorrect prediction affects only the younger buffer lookup. Use the same verified raw word for formatting, pointer forwarding and RVFI.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Add a synchronous word-data and address-tag buffer with one read and one write per cycle. Capture the predicted index alongside the read on EX/MEM advance. Fill on accepted ordinary RAM reads and full-word stores. Invalidate on accepted partial stores. Suppress reuse on a read/write index collision and during the reset validity sweep. Gate training and writes against annulled and trapping instructions. Permit an exact buffer hit to satisfy a read wait while stores and MMIO retain the external handshake.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Generate the predicted row address in parallel with the true effective address. For the LW handoff use the previous buffer candidate's low bits for the prediction, irrespective of tag success. The architectural adder always takes the actual forwarded base or the raw word selected for the older load. Keep all registered memory addresses and alignment checks tied to this architectural sum.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Warm a chain of LW pointers with signed offsets and test it with both buses stalled and poison on unready read data. Force a wrong predicted row by replacing a pointer's buffer entry with an alias. Check the dependent effective address, RVFI raw and extended data, and subsequent store transactions. Include partial-store invalidation, a same-edge RAM collision and reset.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
