{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_11", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor x in map(json.loads,open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl')):\n if x['id'].endswith(('r12s2','r11s2','r9s2')):\n  print(x['title'], x['hypothesis']['hypothesis'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "Return the instruction and its predicted successor from one tagged lookup Use a direct-mapped 1024-row predictor indexed by word PC. Each row\nholds a valid bit, source PC tag, raw instruction, two-bit direction\ncounter and aligned 18-bit target. Train from every valid advancing EX\ninstruction in the low 1 MiB. A normal-PC hit supplies the saved word\non an imem wait and steers the next fetch according to the counter.\nA ready bus always supplies the live word. Read the same table at the\nregistered taken-recovery PC in parallel to cover waits after a miss.\n\nOn a direct branch or JAL, the tagged source PC and identical instruction\nuniquely determine the target recorded during training. Carry one bit\nrecording word equality into EX. On a taken direct transfer, that bit\nverifies the cached target without a 32-bit add followed by a comparison.\nFor zero-offset JALR, compare the forwarded base word address against the\nsaved target independently of alignment. Use sequential prediction for\nnonzero-offset JALR. Resolve every transfer with its actual operands and\nimmediate, including stale predictions and misaligned targets.\n\nA scratch prototype ran ten iterations in 3,414,124 bracketed cycles,\ndown from 3,495,417, with all five canonical CRCs. Gowin placements\nreached 78.348, 80.945 and 80.945 MHz, giving 237.09 iterations/s.\nSelftest and three random programs matched the ISS with ready and\nstalled buses. Acceptance at this cycle count requires a median above\n83.473 MHz. The proposed timing refinement is to compare each JALR\nforwarding candidate to the saved target in parallel. A late bypass\nor WB result select then selects a one-bit match. The measured version\ncompared the target after the 32-bit forwarding mux.\n\nPredict the resident data page for dependent pointer loads Predict that an initialized word offset refers to the currently resident 4 KiB ordinary-RAM page. Learn the page online from repeated accesses. Keep 1024 raw words and a separate one-bit presence RAM. When an aligned LW supplies the next load's base, use th\n…[2527 of 5599 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:fbfbb59ab0256c50]…\ntion a registered source select. Use a banked LUT-RAM or register implementation with a synchronous single-bit set port for accepted fills and full-word stores. The 64-bit mask read needs only the predicted sector index. Keep it independent of register-file data, address addition and fetch-PC selection.\n\nValidate the full resident page, pointer-derived row and same-word write collision before any local completion. Track page invalidation across the lifetime of each snapshot, including stalls. Within one page generation a later fill can only turn an absent bit into a present bit, so an old zero safely uses the bus. A partial store preserves presence and updates only its enabled lanes. On a wrong sector, invalid snapshot or failed validation, use the existing bus retry with the exact address and hold that source through acceptance. Train once per surviving completed RAM load. Measure first-attempt sector matches, timed cycles and median Gowin Fmax. At unchanged cycles, acceptance requires more than 91.42 MHz.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
