{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_98","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261119-003-r13s2.yaml <<'YAML'\nid: hyp-20261119-003-r13s2\ntitle: \\\"Predict loop exits from the phase of earlier invocations\\\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The timed trace contains 529,588 conditional branches. Two individual\n  matrix loop branches each caused 3,241 misses in a per-PC bimodal trace\n  model. Learning the repeated run length reduced each to four. The two-bit\n  counter in the current fetch row has no way to anticipate the final exit\n  after eight consecutive taken outcomes.\nhypothesis: |\n  Learn the number of taken backedges between exits and publish the next\n  direction in the existing tagged fetch row. A 16-entry direct-mapped\n  prototype with binary counts completed ten stalled CoreMark iterations in\n  3,018,909 cycles with all five CRCs, saving 14,906 cycles. At that cycle\n  count a median above 90.97 MHz is needed for acceptance. Keep the loop\n  state and its update off the fetch RAM address path.\n\n  Encode the current iteration as a six-bit shift register starting at zero.\n  Advance it on each taken backward branch by shifting left and inserting\n  the XNOR of bits 5 and 4. Its 63-state cycle identifies each short run\n  length uniquely. On wrap, enter the unused all-ones state and hold it\n  until exit. Remember the phase observed on the last exit. After repeated\n  matches, predict an exit on the next visit when the advanced phase equals\n  the remembered end phase. After a matching exit, predict re-entry as\n  taken. Revoke confidence on an unexpected exit or overrun. The phase\n  update needs one feedback gate and an equality test.\n\n  Read the small training table using the branch PC in EX and consume the\n  registered state with the actual outcome in MEM, including deferred\n  BEQ/BNE. Write the forecast into the existing two direction bits during\n  ordinary row training. Unconfident branches use the existing saturating\n  update. Fetch still looks up one row and EX or MEM resolves the actual\n  branch before wrong-path instructions can retire.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Infer a 16 by 14-bit distributed RAM indexed by word PC bits [5:2].\n      Pack the current phase, last exit phase and two confidence bits as a\n      plain bit vector. Clear during the existing sweep. Train once per\n      valid surviving backward conditional with a representable aligned\n      target. Set confidence to weak after one repeated nonzero bounded\n      length and strong after the next match. On a confident forecast\n      write 00 for exit or 11 for taken. Retain exact PC tagging, live\n      instruction priority and the existing target validation.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Exercise a counted backedge long enough to learn it, then shorten and\n      lengthen the loop. Check the predicted fetch after the last backedge,\n      in-order retirement, store counts and recovery from a wrong forecast.\n      Include an index collision, a run beyond the representable range, a\n      load-dependent branch, imem and dmem waits, and FENCE.I invalidation.\nYAML\npython - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261119-003-r13s2.yaml')\nx=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text());jsonschema.validate(x,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()));print(x['title'])\nprint(3018909*288.08*1.046/1e7)\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
