{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_63", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261119-003-r5s2.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261119-003-r5s2\ntitle: Give predicted fetches and branch recovery separate PC registers\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  Each taken transfer on the current champion discards one fetch slot.\n  The round-3 agree predictor saved 301,155 cycles on its parent. A scratch\n  implementation on the MEM-multiply champion saved 303,485 of its 4,527,760\n  bracketed cycles. With a single fetch PC register, the late EX comparison\n  reached the PC enable and median Fmax was 64.704 MHz. That yielded 153.17\n  iterations/s, below the 154.31 iterations/s needed for acceptance.\nhypothesis: |\n  Predict legal conditional branches and JAL during fetch. Compute both\n  full-width direct successors, PC+4 and PC plus the sign-extended B/J\n  displacement, in parallel with a 64-entry two-bit agree table. Index the\n  table with PC[7:2] XOR PC[13:8]. Its MSB predicts agreement with the\n  backward-taken, forward-untaken rule. Predict eligible JALs taken. Use a\n  direct target only when word-aligned and inside instruction RAM.\n\n  Give fetch two 32-bit PC registers and a one-bit selector. One register\n  captures the successor predicted for the fetch in ID. The other captures\n  the recovery address for the older control transfer in EX, even on a hit.\n  For a direct transfer, EX already holds both possible successors and the\n  saved direction selects the opposite one. For JALR, predict sequentially\n  and use the forwarded base-plus-offset target for recovery. The late\n  comparison drives the selector and squashes ID on a miss. This puts the\n  corrected address on imem immediately after the edge. A correct prediction\n  admits the younger instruction on that edge. Reuse the captured PC+4 as\n  jump-link data via ALU_LUI, removing the link mux after the ALU.\n\n  This prototype took 4,224,275 bracketed cycles for ten iterations with\n  both bus stalls and matched all five canonical CRCs. Gowin's three\n  placement options gave 67.285, 70.714 and 70.714 MHz. The median yields\n  167.40 iterations/s, 13.48% above 147.52. At this cycle count the\n  \n…[2267 of 5339 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:0dfbd0881095a5bf]…\n saved count, incrementing on agreement and decrementing otherwise\n      with two-bit saturation. Keep RVFI pc_next tied to the actual successor.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Wire the EX training write, valid control-transfer flag and recovery\n      address to IF. Feed the same miss to the fetch selector and ID squash.\n      An older dmem wait holds ID/EX. On its releasing edge the miss squashes\n      the younger word and the resolving branch trains once. Keep the existing\n      load/multiply-use and divider interlocks on both predicted paths.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Check consecutive retirements on trained forward and backward taken\n      branches and exact recovery on exit with a wrong-path store. Combine\n      an older blocked load and an instruction wait at correction. Exercise\n      page-crossing JAL, a misaligned taken branch, an untaken out-of-range\n      branch and a forwarded JALR. Check PCs, link values, traps, accepted\n      writes and RVFI order.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
