{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_86", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'ls -l cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261119-003-r6s2.yaml && git status --porcelain=v1 && cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261119-003-r6s2.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "-rw-rw----+ 1 hwebench hwebench 5021 Nov 19 17:41 cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261119-003-r6s2.yaml\nid: hyp-20261119-003-r6s2\ntitle: Select between registered branch successors with a 16-entry agree predictor\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The champion spends one fetch slot on each taken transfer and takes\n  4,527,760 cycles at 78.925 MHz for ten CoreMark iterations. Earlier\n  64-entry predictors recovered about 300,000 cycles. A paired prototype\n  with 64 entries took 4,224,275 cycles at 78.872 MHz. Reducing the table\n  to one native 16-row LUT-RAM cost 21,834 cycles but raised the measured\n  median to 84.253 MHz.\nhypothesis: |\n  Compute PC+4 and the full B/J target in parallel and clock them into\n  separate registers on fetch advance. Clock the predicted direction\n  alongside them. A 16-entry table learns whether a conditional branch\n  agrees with backward-taken, forward-untaken. Predict legal JAL taken.\n  Speculate only to aligned direct targets inside the 1 MiB instruction\n  RAM. JALR uses sequential fetch until EX resolves its forwarded target.\n\n  The two address registers also hold the resolving instruction's possible\n  successors during EX. On a direct miss the saved direction identifies\n  the other address before the forwarded operand comparison finishes.\n  Capture that address in a third register while EX evaluates the branch.\n  The late miss drives a one-bit recovery selector and squashes the\n  younger slot. On a hit, a ready successor can enter ID/EX immediately.\n\n  A scratch implementation on the current champion completed ten iterations\n  in 4,246,109 bracketed cycles with both stall generators and all five\n  canonical CRCs. Gowin placement options 0, 1 and 2 reached 83.094,\n  84.253 and 84.253 MHz. The median gives 198.42 iterations/s, 13.83%\n  above 174.31. This cycle count needs 77.419 MHz to clear the 4.6% margin.\n  Option 0 used 3,328 logic cells, 788 registers and 33 LUT-RAM blocks,\n  one more LUT-RAM block than the champion.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 14\n  confidenc\n…[2061 of 5133 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:0861a2c9f79be60f]…\nports fall-through. Train once per advancing nontrapping\n      conditional branch with its actual agreement, source index and snapshot.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Wire fetch's registered address pair and direction to EX and the EX miss,\n      correction address and training event to fetch. Feed the same miss to\n      hazard_unit.redirect to generate bubble_id. A miss steers fetch\n      even during an older dmem wait. Hold ID/EX and the candidate pair\n      through the wait, then squash the younger slot on release, even if\n      imem is waiting. Qualify training with EX advance.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Check consecutive retirements and link values on a JAL chain. After\n      warm-up, exercise a taken forward branch and a backward loop exit with\n      a wrong-path store. Check accepted write strobes and RVFI PCs and order\n      across an imem wait on dmem release. Exercise an untaken out-of-range\n      target, misaligned taken transfers and a dependent JALR.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
