{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_98", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261119-003-r7s2.yaml && git status --short'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261119-003-r7s2\ntitle: Use branch target buffer hits to predict and replay recurring paths\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  Loop replay supplies words during imem waits, but every taken branch or\n  jump still discards a fetch slot, even when its target word is present.\n  The round-two target buffer saved 313,598 cycles on a core without\n  replay. On today's champion, recognizing a recurring transfer can both\n  predict its successor and start reading resident words along that path.\n  This can cover waits after forward branches, calls and returns as well\n  as short backward branches.\nhypothesis: |\n  Read a tagged 256-entry branch target buffer (BTB) at the registered\n  fetch PC, in parallel with the bus and the existing 64-word replay\n  store. A hit predicts the cached target or PC+4 using a two-bit\n  saturating counter. Accepting either prediction enables replay at the\n  successor. Continue along resident words until an accepted word misses\n  the replay store or EX corrects the path. A fresh BTB hit can start\n  another run. On each fetch the live bus has priority, and replay on a\n  bus wait requires both the full sector tag and that word's presence bit.\n\n  Train legal conditional branches in both directions, and remember the\n  last aligned target of JAL and JALR. Preserve the champion's replay\n  on a cold short backedge. In EX check the saved prediction against\n  the full resolved successor. Register the taken target and fall-through\n  in parallel so a late miss drives a one-bit selector for each address.\n  Feed the BTB from the normal PC register and suppress its lookup on a\n  recovery fetch. This keeps a late EX correction off the RAM read address.\n\n  A scratch implementation ran ten stalled CoreMark iterations in\n  3,731,234 marker-bracketed cycles, down 9.14% from 4,106,616, with all\n  five canonical CRCs matching. Gowin placements reached 72.078, 79.550\n  and 79.550 MHz. The median gives 213.20 iterations/s, 9.19% above\n  195.25. At this cycle count 76.204 MHz clears the acceptance margin.\n  Plac\n…[1969 of 5041 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:7ff848623060e674]…\nve resolved RVFI next PC, link values and trap side effects.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Route prediction recovery to the fetch PC and hazard bubble, and\n      actual taken control flow to loop tracking. Train once per advancing\n      eligible branch or jump with aligned target and both addresses in\n      instruction RAM. Initialize cold conditional counters to 2 on taken\n      or 1 on untaken, saturate a hit's saved counter, and set jumps to 3.\n      Preserve the squash on dmem release and the FENCE.I refetch.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Run beyond the initialization sweep. Check consecutive retirement\n      from a predicted forward JAL into a recorded target block while the\n      bus withholds every word in the block. Exercise a loop exit and a\n      changed JALR target with wrong-path stores, index conflicts, and a\n      FENCE.I followed by changed code at a trained PC. Check accepted\n      store counts, RVFI PCs and order, including across older dmem waits.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
