{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_8", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg -i 'predictor' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python -c 'import json,sys; rows=map(json.loads,sys.stdin); print(\\\"\\\\n\\\".join(r[\\\"id\\\"] + \\\" \\\"+ str(r[\\\"critical_path\\\"]) +\\\"\\\\n\\\"+r[\\\"hypothesis\\\"][\\\"hypothesis\\\"][:2000] for r in rows if r[\\\"category\\\"]==\\\"predictor\\\"))'\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261119-003-r1s2 {'from': 'cpu/u_mem/reg_q.ctrl.mem_to_reg_s0', 'to': 'dmem_2_dmem_2_0_0_s', 'slack_ns': -189.322, 'arrival_ns': 199.616, 'logic_cells': 147, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_wb', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu', 'cpu/u_ex']}\nUse a 256-entry table of two-bit saturating counters indexed by fetch PC[9:2].\nIn parallel with lookup, predecode the fetched word and compute its direct\nB- or J-immediate target. Use the counter MSB for legal conditional branches\nand predict JAL taken. A cold entry predicts backward branches taken and\nforward branches untaken. Allow a speculative target only when word-aligned\nand inside instruction RAM. JALR uses the existing EX target calculation.\n\nAdvance to the predicted successor only when ID accepts the instruction.\nCarry the predicted-taken bit and counter snapshot with that instruction.\nEX knows the exact direct target from the same PC and immediate, so a\ndirection comparison suffices. On a miss, recover to the resolved target or\nPC+4 as appropriate. Train once per advancing, valid, legal conditional\nbranch on its actual outcome. Correct predictions let the successor enter\nID/EX immediately. RVFI reports the resolved PC and existing trap semantics.\n\nhyp-20261119-003-r2s2 {'from': 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu/quotient_q_35_s0', 'to': 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu/remainder_q_0_s0', 'slack_ns': -18.782, 'arrival_ns': 29.076, 'logic_cells': 188, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu']}\nIndex one direct-mapped branch target buffer with fetch PC[9:2]. Pack\nvalid, source tag PC[19:10], target[19:2] and a two-bit saturating counter\ninto a single 31-bit row. For an aligned source in the 1 MiB instruction\nRAM, a valid tag match and a set counter MSB select the cached word target.\nOtherwise fetch PC+4. The same lookup predicts conditional branches, JAL,\nand recurring JALR targets independently of instruction-memory data.\n\nClear one row per clock for the first 256 clocks after reset through the\nordinary write port. Fetch and retirement run normally with sequential\npredictions during initialization. \n…[10183 of 13255 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:c5a7fd4437a21861]…\nl resolved successor. Register the taken target and fall-through\nin parallel so a late miss drives a one-bit selector for each address.\nFeed the BTB from the normal PC register and suppress its lookup on a\nrecovery fetch. This keeps a late EX correction off the RAM read address.\n\nA scratch implementation ran ten stalled CoreMark iterations in\n3,731,234 marker-bracketed cycles, down 9.14% from 4,106,616, with all\nfive canonical CRCs matching. Gowin placements reached 72.078, 79.550\nand 79.550 MHz. The median gives 213.20 iterations/s, 9.19% above\n195.25. At this cycle count 76.204 MHz clears the acceptance margin.\nPlacement zero used 4,034 logic cells and one additional block RAM.\nThe prototype passed all 53 standard formal checks, 96 cocotb cases,\nand the selftest and three random ISS traces with ready and stalled buses.\nA second formal run enabled lookup immediately after reset over arbitrary\ninitial BTB contents to exercise warm predictions within the proof depth.\nAll 53 checks passed in that run as well.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
