{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_13", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor a in list(map(json.loads,open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl')))[33:37]:\n h=a['hypothesis']\n print(a['title'],h['hypothesis'][:2000])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "Predict the resident data page for dependent pointer loads Predict that an initialized word offset refers to the currently resident 4 KiB ordinary-RAM page. Learn the page online from repeated accesses. Keep 1024 raw words and a separate one-bit presence RAM. When an aligned LW supplies the next load's base, use the unchecked buffered pointer plus the younger displacement to launch its word and presence lookups. For other loads use the forwarded base plus displacement. Register one bit certifying that the index came from an ordinary forwarded base or from a pointer that the older load actually supplied locally. Hold this information with the load across waits.\n\nA present offset predicts a local completion in MEM. Use that source prediction to compute the dependent pointer address while independently verifying the page, row provenance, alignment and absence of a read/write collision. If verification fails, hold the older load and its consumer, latch a retry bit and obtain the word on the external bus. Keep the request stable to acceptance. A verified local load advances while the bus is unready. The same completing raw word feeds the pointer handoff, load formatting and RVFI.\n\nA scratch prototype took 3,033,815 bracketed cycles for ten iterations and matched all five CRCs. Three Gowin placements reached 82.715, 83.077 and 83.077 MHz for 273.84 iterations/s, 2.58% above the champion. Starting with an empty page selector preserved the cycle count and passed all 59 pipeline tests. Its three placements reached 79.201, 80.966 and 80.966 MHz, yielding 266.88 iterations/s. Acceptance at that cycle count requires more than 84.710 MHz. Keep the 12-bit speculative index adder separate from the three architectural address candidates. Sharing its inputs with the full-width sums reduced median Fmax to 81.171 MHz in a paired run.\n\nStore prescaled reciprocal seeds for one Newton divider iteration Replace the two dependent radix-8 trials with a synchronous reciprocal\nROM and one fixed-point Newton iteration. Store each seed at it\n…[4107 of 7179 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:4ad804df88e2b5df]…\ntion a registered source select. Use a banked LUT-RAM or register implementation with a synchronous single-bit set port for accepted fills and full-word stores. The 64-bit mask read needs only the predicted sector index. Keep it independent of register-file data, address addition and fetch-PC selection.\n\nValidate the full resident page, pointer-derived row and same-word write collision before any local completion. Track page invalidation across the lifetime of each snapshot, including stalls. Within one page generation a later fill can only turn an absent bit into a present bit, so an old zero safely uses the bus. A partial store preserves presence and updates only its enabled lanes. On a wrong sector, invalid snapshot or failed validation, use the existing bus retry with the exact address and hold that source through acceptance. Train once per surviving completed RAM load. Measure first-attempt sector matches, timed cycles and median Gowin Fmax. At unchanged cycles, acceptance requires more than 91.42 MHz.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor i in [28,31,32]:\n a=list(map(json.loads,open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl')))[i]\n print(a['title'])\n print(a['hypothesis']['hypothesis'],*[x['description'] for x in a['changes']])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_14", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor i in [28,31,32]:\n a=list(map(json.loads,open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl')))[i]\n print(a['title'])\n print(a['hypothesis']['hypothesis'],*[x['description'] for x in a['changes']])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "Replace serial divider trials with two reciprocal quotient estimates\nEstimate an unsigned quotient twice with the same conservative\nreciprocal, first on the dividend and then on the residual. Two\nregistered DSP products per estimate replace the pair of serial\nradix-8 trial banks. A 2,048-word reciprocal ROM bounds the error\ntightly enough that one final comparison gives the exact quotient\nand remainder. Keep the eight-cycle EX handshake.\n\nLet H = 2^32 and let A and D be the operand magnitudes. For D >= 2,\nchoose the smallest shift s such that j = (D-1) >> s fits in 11 bits.\nRead floor(H/(j+1)) from ROM and shift the word right by s to obtain M.\nThis equals floor(H/U) for U = (j+1) << s, the upper endpoint of D's\nbucket. Each estimate q = high32(x*M) is a lower bound on floor(x/D).\nApply it to x=A, subtract q0*D, then apply it to that residual and\nsubtract q1*D. If the final residual is at least D, subtract D once\nand increment q0+q1. Apply the architectural sign to the selected result.\n\nA scratch implementation reached 86.266, 92.151 and 92.151 MHz across\nGowin's three placement options. Stalled CoreMark passed all five CRCs\nin 3,414,124 bracketed cycles. The median gives 269.91 iterations/s,\n10.04% above the champion. The design used 15 BSRAMs and six DSPs,\nup from 11 and two. The median placement's worst path now runs from\nwriteback to the EX/MEM ALU result. The ALU and pipeline suites and\ndirected reciprocal-boundary tests passed on the scratch RTL.\n Capture A, D-1 and the sign and remainder flags on issue. D-1 is\n~b for a negative signed divisor and b-1 otherwise. In phase 1\nreconstruct D, synchronously read the 2048x32 block ROM and\nregister s. Select j and s together by priority on the highest\nset bit of D-1, defaulting to its low 11 bits and shift zero.\nInitialize ROM constants with 64-bit arithmetic in two blocks\nof 1024 entries. Index zero can store zero because D=1 is handled\nexplicitly. Register M in phase 2.\n\nUse one unsigned high-product datapath for phases 3 and 5 and one\nlow-product datapath for ph\n…[6895 of 9967 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:1b9cf9471b4a6091]…\nt into the\nsame 20-bit register.\n\nRead the 4096x32 reciprocal ROM in phase 1. Initialize it in four\nblocks of 1024 to fit Gowin's synthesis loop limit. Use separate\nunsigned DSP product paths. Register high32(A*reciprocal) in phase\n2, low32(q*D[11:0]) in phase 3 and A-q*D in phase 4. Use the borrow\nfrom R-D in phase 5 to choose the corrected quotient or remainder.\nHandle D=0 and D=1 in phase 6. In phase 7 select the small result\nor the large-path quotient or remainder and apply its sign. Preserve\nsigned remainder, INT_MIN/-1, DIV by zero returning all ones, and\nREM by zero returning the original dividend. Keep the busy/done\nhandshake and ALTOPS operations on their existing edges.\n Check all four operations on either side of 4096, including maximum\nunsigned operands, both signs and values one below, at and above\nmultiples. Exercise all sixteen large-path digits and both outcomes\nof the small-path correction. Alternate paths on consecutive requests\nand retain the exact completion-edge and input-change checks.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
