{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"rg -n 'predictor|bimodal|loop exits|replay|redirect' cores/bench/LESSONS.md\"","aggregated_output":"3:- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-003-r1s2 (regression, +7.76%): Correctly predicting a taken branch or JAL in fetch avoided a one-cycle EX redirect bubble, but the combinational DIV/REM path still limited CoreMark throughput to 13.06 iterations/s versus 96.04 with the six-round divider.\n7:- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-001-r3s0 (improvement, +16.18%): Clearing only ID/EX valid on a bubble let load-use detection read the raw fetch word in parallel with EX redirect, while registering the JALR adder's sum for loads and stores bypassed the ALU result mux on the memory address path.\n9:- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-003-r3s2 (regression, +10.89%): Counting agreement with the displacement sign lets taken backward branches and untaken forward branches reinforce the same counter, so 64 entries preserved the 256-entry predictor’s roughly 7% CoreMark cycle savings, though throughput trailed the round winner at 121.58 versus 127.38 iterations/s.\n11:- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-003-r4s2 (regression, +9.92%): Routing fetch PC+4 through ALU_LUI for JAL/JALR removed the jump-link mux from EX/MEM.alu_result and raised median Fmax by 5.24 MHz at the same CoreMark cycle count in paired page-local predictor prototypes, though the final build trailed the MEM-stage multiplier at 140.01 versus 147.52 iterations/s.\n15:- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-002-r6s1 (improvement, +12.01%): After a taken backward branch of up to 256 bytes, replaying issued ID/EX words on an exact fetch PC match let decode issue while imemReady was low, cutting the prototype's ten-iteration CoreMark cycle count by 9.3%.\n16:- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-003-r6s2 (regression, +10.83%): In paired prototypes, shrinking the agree predictor from 64 entries to 16 let its counters fit in one Gowin LUT-RAM and raised median Fmax by 6.8%, outweighing a 0.52% increase in CoreMark cycles.\n18:- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-002-r7s1 (regression, +6.26%): Probing a 128-entry replay table on every fetch let ID/EX accept saved controls and immediates at jump targets and loop exits during instruction-bus waits, cutting prototype CoreMark cycles by 4.98% versus replay limited to short backward loops.\n19:- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-003-r7s2 (improvement, +10.56%): Starting replay at the target or fall-through predicted by a BTB hit let fetch serve resident words during instruction-bus waits, saving 83,953 cycles across ten CoreMark iterations versus the same predictor with replay triggered only by short backedges.\n20:- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-001-r8s0 (improvement, +8.28%): Packing each PC tag with its instruction in a 512-row block RAM limited a conflicting replay fill to one eviction, while parallel reads at the registered normal and recovery PCs kept the recovery mux off both RAM address paths.\n21:- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-002-r8s1 (regression, +7.48%): Reading successor RAM during the predecessor’s fetch let a PC-matched word cover instruction-bus waits even across predicted jumps, cutting prototype CoreMark cycles by 8.03%, though final throughput trailed the 512-entry replay table at 232.01 versus 233.74 iterations/s.\n22:- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-003-r8s2 (regression, +6.78%): Comparing EX rd with rs1 and rs2 of the bus, successor and replay words in parallel with the successor PC check kept the instruction mux off the load-use hazard path, raising median Fmax from 65.969 to 77.982 MHz at the same CoreMark cycle count in paired prototypes.\n37:- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261119-003-r13s2 (regression, +0.14%): Matching backedge phase to a learned exit phase let the fetch row anticipate fixed-trip loop exits, saving 14,906 cycles over ten stalled CoreMark iterations, but divider feedback limited median Fmax to 87.09 MHz against a 90.97 MHz acceptance target.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
