{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_8", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat test/cosim/build.sh; cat test/cosim/main.cpp | head -225; command -v riscv32-unknown-elf-gcc; ls -l cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses | tail -4; rg -n -i 'fmax|seed|cosim_build|bench.*sim' tools/eval/fpga.py | head -80\"", "aggregated_output": "#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Build the Verilator-based cosim binary that drives `core` against an ELF\n# program. Produces $OBJ_DIR/cosim_sim (default: test/cosim/obj_dir/cosim_sim).\n#\n# RTL_DIR (default: rtl/) is globbed dynamically (with core_pkg.sv forced\n# first because its compilation-unit-scope typedefs and localparams must be\n# visible before any module references them). Hypotheses are allowed to add,\n# rename, or delete files inside rtl/, so a hardcoded file list would\n# silently break restructuring hypotheses.\nset -euo pipefail\n\nREPO_ROOT=\"$(cd \"$(dirname \"${BASH_SOURCE[0]}\")/../..\" && pwd)\"\nCOSIM_DIR=\"$REPO_ROOT/test/cosim\"\nif [ -z \"${RTL_DIR:-}\" ] || [ -z \"${OBJ_DIR:-}\" ]; then\n  echo \"ERROR: test/cosim/build.sh requires RTL_DIR and OBJ_DIR env vars.\" >&2\n  echo \"  Example: RTL_DIR=cores/v1/rtl OBJ_DIR=cores/v1/obj_dir bash test/cosim/build.sh\" >&2\n  exit 2\nfi\n\n# NRET picks which RVFI port set main.cpp expects on `core`.\n# 1 = single-issue (only `_0` ports); 2 = dual-channel (both `_0` and `_1`).\n# Default 2 preserves existing behavior; orchestrator sets to 1 for cores\n# whose core.yaml declares nret: 1.\nNRET=\"${NRET:-2}\"\n\n# Ensure OSS CAD Suite tools are on PATH for non-interactive shells.\nTOOLCHAIN=\"$REPO_ROOT/.toolchain\"\nif [ -d \"$TOOLCHAIN/oss-cad-suite/bin\" ]; then\n  export PATH=\"$TOOLCHAIN/oss-cad-suite/bin:$PATH\"\nfi\n\nmkdir -p \"$OBJ_DIR\"\n\n# Glob $RTL_DIR/*.sv. core_pkg.sv first; the rest in a stable lexicographic\n# order (modulo case-insensitive sort, which Verilator/gcc don't care\n# about). If core_pkg.sv is missing the build will catch that downstream\n# via undefined-typedef errors.\nRTL_FILES=()\n[ -f \"$RTL_DIR/core_pkg.sv\" ] && RTL_FILES+=(\"$RTL_DIR/core_pkg.sv\")\nfor f in \"$RTL_DIR\"/*.sv; do\n  [ \"$(basename \"$f\")\" = \"core_pkg.sv\" ] && continue\n  RTL_FILES+=(\"$f\")\ndone\n\nif [ ${#RTL_FILES[@]} -eq 0 ]; then\n  echo \"ERROR: no rtl/*.sv files found.\" >&2\n  exit 1\nfi\n\nverilator --cc --exe --build \\\n  -Mdir \"$OBJ_DIR\" \\\n  \"+incdir+$RTL_DIR\" \\\n  --top-module core \\\n  -Wall -Wno-fatal -Wno-style \\\n  -CFLAGS \"-DN\n…[12932 of 16004 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:e0b7139dde5515bf]…\nr_size: {uart[-500:]}'\n290:        ('seedcrc',   _uart_int(uart, r'seedcrc\\s*:\\s*0x([0-9a-fA-F]+)', 16)),\n314:def measure_fmax(worktree: str, target: str, pairs=None) -> dict:\n315:    \"\"\"Fmax only (no CoreMark), for the final champion's headline score.\n318:    Returns {'fmax_mhz': float | None, 'pairs': [[option, fmax, levels]...],\n325:    return {'fmax_mhz': summ.get('fmax_mhz'),\n326:            'pairs': [[r['place_option'], r.get('fmax_mhz'), r.get('levels')] for r in res],\n332:    Vendor place-and-route (tools/eval/gowin.py) for Fmax and area, and the\n340:        'fmax_mhz': float,               — median over gowin.PLACE_OPTIONS\n342:        'fitness': float,                — iter/sec = fmax_hz * iter/cycle\n344:        'seeds': [float, ...],           — Fmax per placement option\n370:                'fmax_mhz': fp['fmax_mhz'], 'seeds': fp['seeds']}\n377:            'fmax_mhz': fp['fmax_mhz'],\n378:            'seeds': fp['seeds'],\n382:    fitness = fp['fmax_mhz'] * cm['iter_per_cycle'] * 1_000_000  # iter/sec\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor l in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(l)\n if d['id'].endswith(('r10s2','r11s2','r12s2','r13s2','r10s1')):\n  print(d['id'],d['motivation'],d['hypothesis'].get('hypothesis',''))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_9", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor l in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(l)\n if d['id'].endswith(('r10s2','r11s2','r12s2','r13s2','r10s1')):\n  print(d['id'],d['motivation'],d['hypothesis'].get('hypothesis',''))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261119-002-r10s1 The champion spends 3,414,124 cycles on ten CoreMark iterations. Its\ndecode interlock charges an immediate consumer a cycle even when it needs\nthe missing value only for a comparison, an addition or store data. In a\nscratch run, 136,574 deferred branches were BEQ/BNE and 36,934 deferred\ninteger operations were adds. Dependent loads accounted for a further\n61,104 interlocked cycles after specializing for these frequent consumers.\n Give the in-order pipeline a second execution point for dependent\nADD/ADDI, BEQ/BNE and store data. Allow these consumers to issue behind\na load, multiply or deferred add. EX tags the missing sources and captures\nthe available operand. In MEM a single adder or equality comparator pairs\nit with registered WB data. Consecutive dependent adds can issue every\ncycle. A store with a ready base can accept its data at the store formatter.\n\nFor a load whose base comes from the immediately preceding aligned LW,\nforward the accepted raw word into address generation. Capture the sum\nwith the younger load's signed displacement in EX/MEM.mem_addr on the\nsame edge that sends the older word to WB. This sustains dependent pointer\nloads and gives the external data RAM a registered address. Other operations\nthat need an unavailable operand in EX use the existing interlock.\n\nA scratch prototype completed ten stalled iterations in 3,182,385 cycles,\n6.79% fewer than the champion, with all five canonical CRCs. Restricting\nload-address forwarding to zero displacement took 3,213,302 cycles.\nRegistering the LW producer class and selecting the address base before\na shared adder reached 80.399, 81.554 and 81.554 MHz on Gowin. The median\ngives 256.27 iterations/s. Acceptance requires more than 256.56, or\n81.6482 MHz at this cycle count. Fold the class and source match into a\nsingle registered handoff bit to remove the last select qualification\nfrom address generation.\n\nhyp-20261119-003-r10s2 Of 544,852 loads in the champion's timed CoreMark trace, 204,039 have\na destination matching a source f\n…[7753 of 10825 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:e63b4faceb8c0205]…\ne current iteration as a six-bit shift register starting at zero.\nAdvance it on each taken backward branch by shifting left and inserting\nthe XNOR of bits 5 and 4. Its 63-state cycle identifies each short run\nlength uniquely. On wrap, enter the unused all-ones state and hold it\nuntil exit. Remember the phase observed on the last exit. After repeated\nmatches, predict an exit on the next visit when the advanced phase equals\nthe remembered end phase. After a matching exit, predict re-entry as\ntaken. Revoke confidence on an unexpected exit or overrun. The phase\nupdate needs one feedback gate and an equality test.\n\nRead the small training table using the branch PC in EX and consume the\nregistered state with the actual outcome in MEM, including deferred\nBEQ/BNE. Write the forecast into the existing two direction bits during\nordinary row training. Unconfident branches use the existing saturating\nupdate. Fetch still looks up one row and EX or MEM resolves the actual\nbranch before wrong-path instructions can retire.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
