{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_63","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261120-003-r14s2.yaml <<'YAML'\nid: hyp-20261120-003-r14s2\ntitle: Look up load-correlated branch exceptions one fetch ahead\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The champion executes 529,588 conditional branches in ten timed iterations.\n  In the state-machine kernel several branches classify the same loaded\n  character. At PC 0x2260 the per-PC bimodal model missed 3,120 of 16,240\n  visits. The low byte of the latest retired load distinguishes many of\n  these visits and is available before most of the comparisons execute.\n  The address and character values in this example are observations only.\n  The hardware learns correlations for any branch PC and any load value.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add a load-value-indexed exception table to the current bimodal predictor.\n  Learn a direction for a particular branch and load context when the base\n  counter predicts incorrectly. Fetch the exception entry one instruction\n  early by assuming the queried branch follows the current word sequentially.\n  This lets a synchronous block RAM deliver its counter and signature before\n  the branch needs its target. A matched exception selects the direction.\n  The ordinary tagged fetch row supplies the instruction and direct target.\n  The base counter continues to train from actual outcomes.\n  \n  A scratch implementation with 256 entries completed ten iterations in\n  3,007,805 marker-bracketed cycles with both buses stalled and all five CRCs\n  correct. The champion takes 3,033,815 cycles. At the prototype cycle\n  count acceptance requires a median above 90.63 MHz. Direction accuracy\n  alone cannot close that gap. Keep the signature comparison and the\n  exception read away from the wide successor address mux.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      For current normal word PC P, index 256 rows by XOR of P[9:2],\n      P[17:10] and the low byte C of the last retired load. Store a valid\n      bit, signature C XOR P[17:10], and a two-bit counter. At branch\n      training address the table with branch word address minus one and the\n      saved C. Enable the forecast on an accepted sequential transition.\n      On a verified fetch row for an eligible direct conditional, select\n      the exception counter on a signature hit. Train on a hit or a base\n      miss. Invalidate on reset and FENCE.I and keep live-bus priority.\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: |\n      Carry the actual fetch direction, context byte, exception hit and old\n      counter with the branch through ID/EX and EX/MEM. Retain the separate\n      original bimodal counter for training.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Use the recorded direction for early correction and pass it to the\n      existing deferred-branch checker. Pass the fetch-time training fields\n      across EX/MEM holds and replays.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Send the low eight bits of the architecturally formatted WB load result\n      to the predictor on a valid nontrapping load retirement. Train once on\n      a surviving conditional in MEM, including a deferred BEQ or BNE.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Drive the same conditional after two different load values. Check\n      direction learning and both recoveries with an alias, a changed code\n      word, a wrong-path store, bus waits and FENCE.I.\nYAML\nPYTHONPATH=\"'$PWD python \"$TMPDIR/stats.py\"'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
