{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_148", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261120-003-r14s2.yaml <<'YAML'\nid: hyp-20261120-003-r14s2\ntitle: Let retiring loads predict the direction of later conditional branches\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  CoreMark's state parser tests one loaded character several times. In a\n  retirement-trace model, the branch at 0x2260 alone defeated a per-PC\n  bimodal counter on 3,120 of 16,240 visits. The load's PC and returned\n  value distinguish visits that share a branch PC. Reading a prediction\n  table when the load retires gives fetch that evidence before it asks\n  for a branch direction. The hardware learns the association online.\nhypothesis: |\n  Use each retiring load to read four branch forecasts from a small\n  exception table. Hash byte-address bits [9:2] and [17:10] of the load\n  PC with the low byte of its formatted WB result to select one of 256\n  rows. Each bank holds a valid bit, ten bits of branch PC and a two-bit\n  direction counter. Latch all four records until the next load retires.\n  In normal fetch, PC[3:2] selects a bank and PC[13:4] identifies the\n  branch. Apply an override only on an exact instruction-row hit for a\n  conditional with an aligned, representable target. Keep training the\n  base counter independently. Allocate an exception on a base miss and\n  update a matched exception from the actual direction.\n  \n  Use the improved taken forecast to shorten recovery timing. For a\n  predicted-taken conditional, register the EX outcome and check it in\n  MEM. A wrong forecast, including a stale direct target, uses the existing\n  late redirect and annul path. Predicted-fallthrough branches with ready\n  operands retain the early taken redirect. A BEQ or BNE waiting for a WB\n  operand still compares in MEM. This removes the forwarded conditional\n  comparison from the EX fallthrough-redirect signal.\n  \n  A four-bank scratch prototype retired the canonical workload in\n  3,011,264 marker-bracketed cycles with both bus stalls, all five CRCs\n  and the validation banner. Gowin placements reached 84.679, 89.198\n  and 89.198 MHz. The median yields 296.21 iterations/s, a 2.82 percent\n  gain over 288.08. Acceptance at that cycle count needs more than\n  90.739 MHz. Compare the four registered signatures in parallel and\n  select the one-bit match with PC[3:2] to reduce steering delay.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Implement four synchronous 256-by-13 banks. Read on load commitment\n      and train at the branch's saved load index. Invalidate on reset and\n      FENCE.I. Record the chosen direction on the same acceptance edge\n      that steers fetch. Preserve instruction identity, live-bus priority\n      and operand-demand decoding through stalls and recovery.\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: |\n      Carry the chosen direction, original base counter, eight-bit load\n      index, context validity, exception hit and exception counter with\n      the branch. Distinguish an EX outcome awaiting validation from a\n      branch that still needs a WB operand for comparison.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Supply the valid nontrapping WB load PC and result byte. Keep\n      training in order, once per surviving conditional with its original\n      lookup context. Use the existing younger DIV and FENCE.I interlock\n      whenever branch verification extends into MEM.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Capture the actual early outcome, target and fetch decision for a\n      predicted-taken conditional. Suppress its EX redirect and qualify\n      it for the MEM check. Retain jump resolution and alignment traps.\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Check the recorded direction and target identity. Select the\n      registered EX outcome or resolve equality using the required WB\n      operand. On a miss redirect to the architectural successor and\n      annul younger side effects before they reach memory or retirement.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Exercise one branch after two load values that demand opposite\n      directions. Check both repair paths, PC and context aliases, a\n      replaced instruction, FENCE.I and dmem waits. Assert exact RVFI\n      order, trap behavior and accepted store counts on recovery.\nYAML\npython - <<'PY'\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema,pathlib\np=pathlib.Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261120-003-r14s2.yaml')\nd=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text());jsonschema.validate(d,json.load(open('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json')))\nassert d['id']==p.stem and d['category']=='predictor' and all((pathlib.Path('cores/bench')/c['file']).is_file() for c in d['changes'])\nprint('validated',p)\nPY\nrg -n 'failed|FAIL|PASSED|passed' \\\"\"'$TMPDIR/test-asym4.log\" | tail -8; PYTHONPATH=$PWD python \"$TMPDIR/stats.py\" | rg '\"'asym|antecip|partial'\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
